1
00:01:03,015 --> 00:01:07,094
- One more day and you're free, Alan.
- Yeah, I'll miss all this study time.

2
00:01:07,095 --> 00:01:08,535
Yeah, I bet.

3
00:01:12,495 --> 00:01:15,014
- You're a dead man, Baylis.
- You can't touch me.

4
00:01:15,015 --> 00:01:16,085
(GRUNTING)

5
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:18,706
GUARD: He's got a rope!

6
00:01:19,255 --> 00:01:21,775
(ALARM BLARING)

7
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:23,455
(GRUNTING)

8
00:01:23,775 --> 00:01:26,503
(OFFICeR SHOUTING IN DISTANCe)

9
00:01:35,055 --> 00:01:36,854
You have a pre-trial next.

10
00:01:36,855 --> 00:01:40,735
A Mrs Hurst.
She wants to sue the council.

11
00:01:40,815 --> 00:01:42,774
- Over what?
- She adopted a wrong 'un,

12
00:01:42,775 --> 00:01:44,294
only nobody told her, it seems.

13
00:01:44,295 --> 00:01:46,854
This is her third attempt, Judge.

14
00:01:46,855 --> 00:01:50,334
You threw it out a year ago.
Her papers weren't filed correctly.

15
00:01:50,335 --> 00:01:53,881
Ask the Presider to take it, would you?

16
00:01:57,215 --> 00:01:59,761
Well, I can't hear it, John.

17
00:02:00,055 --> 00:02:02,415
I've got her adopted son.

18
00:02:02,495 --> 00:02:05,414
The schoolboy who raped his teacher.

19
00:02:05,415 --> 00:02:08,506
I could let you take that instead.

20
00:02:09,695 --> 00:02:10,895
Vera?

21
00:02:11,695 --> 00:02:15,374
My wife's insisting on sitting
on the bench with me on this one.

22
00:02:15,375 --> 00:02:16,935
Quirky privilege.

23
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:18,814
(VIDeO GAMe BeePING)

24
00:02:18,815 --> 00:02:19,934
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

25
00:02:19,935 --> 00:02:21,415
JO: Come in.

26
00:02:24,215 --> 00:02:27,654
- And how's our new pupil doing?
- He's a star.

27
00:02:27,655 --> 00:02:29,614
Bellman have been on again, miss,

28
00:02:29,615 --> 00:02:33,054
asking if I persuaded you
to take on the Alan Ferns defence.

29
00:02:33,055 --> 00:02:34,414
I can't see a defence.

30
00:02:34,415 --> 00:02:38,534
He was caught on a CCTV camera.
He should plead guilty.

31
00:02:38,535 --> 00:02:40,445
The thing is, miss,

32
00:02:40,535 --> 00:02:43,094
Bellman have a huge
fraud case coming up.

33
00:02:43,095 --> 00:02:44,254
A private payer.

34
00:02:44,255 --> 00:02:45,934
Chambers would do very nicely from it.

35
00:02:45,935 --> 00:02:48,754
Yes, I know the pressure, Tony.

36
00:02:50,415 --> 00:02:51,894
TONY: The prisoner did ask for you.

37
00:02:51,895 --> 00:02:53,934
Hmm. Should I feel flattered?

38
00:02:53,935 --> 00:02:57,254
He's very bright, miss.
He's taken two law degrees in prison.

39
00:02:57,255 --> 00:03:01,437
Well, then he should know
he hasn't got a case.

40
00:03:01,615 --> 00:03:04,534
I'll think about it.
Talk it over with Michael.

41
00:03:04,535 --> 00:03:07,354
That's all right. I don't mind.

42
00:03:09,095 --> 00:03:12,641
KADANS: Oh, hello. Nice of you to come.

43
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:17,094
There is no defence, Mr Ferns.

44
00:03:17,095 --> 00:03:19,774
Your best option
would be a deal with the CPS.

45
00:03:19,775 --> 00:03:22,014
And what about provocation?

46
00:03:22,015 --> 00:03:24,534
Is there evidence
the victim provoked you?

47
00:03:24,535 --> 00:03:29,717
He abused boys no older
than the one you're looking after.

48
00:03:31,935 --> 00:03:34,454
I have no regrets, Mrs Mills.

49
00:03:34,455 --> 00:03:37,274
Baylis claimed he was innocent.

50
00:03:37,415 --> 00:03:41,961
Well, we know of at least
eight children he abused.

51
00:03:42,095 --> 00:03:43,574
The provocation
would need to have produced

52
00:03:43,575 --> 00:03:46,094
a pretty instant loss of self-control.

53
00:03:46,095 --> 00:03:47,914
He got three months.

54
00:03:47,935 --> 00:03:50,094
Half the fathers in the country
probably wanted to kill him.

55
00:03:50,095 --> 00:03:51,914
Still not a defence.

56
00:03:52,695 --> 00:03:56,334
Well, people are saying I'm mad
to do what I did.

57
00:03:56,335 --> 00:03:59,254
I only had a day
of my own sentence left to serve.

58
00:03:59,255 --> 00:04:02,054
Diminished responsibility
could be a runner.

59
00:04:02,055 --> 00:04:04,534
If we can show that
you were suffering from an abnormality

60
00:04:04,535 --> 00:04:07,334
that impaired
your mental responsibility.

61
00:04:07,335 --> 00:04:08,614
Well, I didn't lose all reason.

62
00:04:08,615 --> 00:04:11,134
Then what would you hope
to convince a jury of?

63
00:04:11,135 --> 00:04:13,574
That I acted in defence of others.

64
00:04:13,575 --> 00:04:15,805
To prevent a crime?

65
00:04:15,895 --> 00:04:17,375
Is that it?

66
00:04:17,455 --> 00:04:19,734
- Barry Baylis was in prison.
- Yeah.

67
00:04:19,735 --> 00:04:24,934
But he'd have got out in a few weeks
and he'd have preyed on other children.

68
00:04:24,935 --> 00:04:29,014
Well, despite his passionate belief,
I'm not sure he has any genuine defence.

69
00:04:29,015 --> 00:04:30,470
Oh, come on, Jo.

70
00:04:30,975 --> 00:04:33,814
Remember that speech you gave
to the local Law Society,

71
00:04:33,815 --> 00:04:36,725
"Defence is part of the process.

72
00:04:36,815 --> 00:04:38,294
"In our legal system,

73
00:04:38,295 --> 00:04:42,894
"every prisoner deserves
the best possible defence, regardless."

74
00:04:42,895 --> 00:04:46,014
- Where'd you dig that up?
- I didn't.

75
00:04:46,015 --> 00:04:48,325
Alan Ferns did.

76
00:04:58,855 --> 00:05:01,128
- Hello.
- Michael: Hello.

77
00:05:02,375 --> 00:05:04,654
Hello, Mrs Tredaway.
Did we have an appointment?

78
00:05:04,655 --> 00:05:06,135
No.

79
00:05:06,215 --> 00:05:07,854
How are you getting on
with the cello, Michael?

80
00:05:07,855 --> 00:05:09,255
Oh, all right.

81
00:05:09,335 --> 00:05:12,135
Can... Can we talk inside?

82
00:05:12,215 --> 00:05:13,415
Of course.

83
00:05:13,495 --> 00:05:14,950
(CAR LOCK BeePS)

84
00:05:26,495 --> 00:05:29,174
His teacher says he could be very good.

85
00:05:29,175 --> 00:05:30,695
He is already.

86
00:05:34,015 --> 00:05:35,375
Mrs Mills.

87
00:05:38,215 --> 00:05:42,374
A man who thinks he's Michael's father
has made contact with the council.

88
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:43,575
What?

89
00:05:44,135 --> 00:05:45,534
Is that possible?

90
00:05:45,535 --> 00:05:48,774
He says he had a brief affair
with Diana Hulsey.

91
00:05:48,775 --> 00:05:50,895
The time would fit.

92
00:05:50,975 --> 00:05:52,614
He's come all the way from South Africa.

93
00:05:52,615 --> 00:05:54,654
On the off-chance that he might be?

94
00:05:54,655 --> 00:05:57,934
- He'd like to meet Michael.
- He can't.

95
00:05:57,935 --> 00:06:00,854
I mean, even if he is the father,
he's had nothing to do with him.

96
00:06:00,855 --> 00:06:06,128
He says that was less to do with him
than Michael's mother.

97
00:06:06,255 --> 00:06:08,437
Does he have any rights?

98
00:06:09,415 --> 00:06:14,735
If his DNA test is positive,
then we'd encourage contact.

99
00:06:14,815 --> 00:06:17,452
And where does that leave me?

100
00:06:21,135 --> 00:06:22,335
Yes.

101
00:06:34,575 --> 00:06:37,121
I couldn't bear to lose him.

102
00:06:39,055 --> 00:06:42,254
- Does Michael know about this man?
- No, I haven't told him yet.

103
00:06:42,255 --> 00:06:44,894
DeeD: He might not be his father.

104
00:06:44,895 --> 00:06:47,168
I've got a feeling he is.

105
00:06:47,655 --> 00:06:50,383
If he is, you could fight him.

106
00:06:53,615 --> 00:06:55,255
Oh, I feel sick.

107
00:06:56,975 --> 00:06:59,248
You haven't lost him yet.

108
00:07:00,415 --> 00:07:02,506
What should I do, John?

109
00:07:04,775 --> 00:07:07,775
How might Michael feel about him?

110
00:07:07,975 --> 00:07:13,066
How would you have felt
if your real father had shown up?

111
00:07:16,855 --> 00:07:20,334
My lord, the case we're asking you
to set down for trial

112
00:07:20,335 --> 00:07:22,935
is clear and unequivocal.

113
00:07:23,815 --> 00:07:30,335
Under Regulation 12 (1 ) of
the Adoption Agencies Regulations 1983,

114
00:07:30,415 --> 00:07:32,894
it's appended to my skeleton
in your bundle,

115
00:07:32,895 --> 00:07:37,532
Rotherwas Council has a duty
to provide the adopter,

116
00:07:37,575 --> 00:07:39,854
Mrs Hurst and her husband,

117
00:07:39,855 --> 00:07:42,734
with written information about,
among other things,

118
00:07:42,735 --> 00:07:45,054
the personal history of the child.

119
00:07:45,055 --> 00:07:50,054
We would argue that what they left out
of the reports on Paul Lawrence,

120
00:07:50,055 --> 00:07:54,295
who was 11 years, eight months
at the time,

121
00:07:54,375 --> 00:07:58,134
was deliberately misleading
as to his true character.

122
00:07:58,135 --> 00:07:59,974
Do you have evidence that indicates

123
00:07:59,975 --> 00:08:03,254
that the council
painted an untrue picture?

124
00:08:03,255 --> 00:08:05,774
Well, we will be seeking
Your Lordship's permission

125
00:08:05,775 --> 00:08:07,614
to search the council's database.

126
00:08:07,615 --> 00:08:09,814
So far, they have refused
to provide us with the...

127
00:08:09,815 --> 00:08:11,454
No, my lord, to do so

128
00:08:11,455 --> 00:08:14,534
would put the council in breach
of the Data Protection Act.

129
00:08:14,535 --> 00:08:16,534
Well, if there is any evidence
to support his claim,

130
00:08:16,535 --> 00:08:18,374
the council can't resist it.

131
00:08:18,375 --> 00:08:21,574
The council is protecting some of
the most vulnerable members of society

132
00:08:21,575 --> 00:08:22,894
by its refusal.

133
00:08:22,895 --> 00:08:27,054
DeeD: Let's see if Mr Stafford Clark
makes his case first, shall we?

134
00:08:27,055 --> 00:08:29,055
I'd like to hear that.

135
00:08:29,895 --> 00:08:32,894
The council's duty was breached first

136
00:08:32,895 --> 00:08:35,574
by an adoption officer,
Margaret Tredaway,

137
00:08:35,575 --> 00:08:38,014
concealing information
about Paul Lawrence,

138
00:08:38,015 --> 00:08:39,854
second, in destroying it.

139
00:08:39,855 --> 00:08:42,614
My lord, counsel for the claimant
is offering as fact

140
00:08:42,615 --> 00:08:44,654
that which is merely hypothesis.

141
00:08:44,655 --> 00:08:46,655
He may well have fact.

142
00:08:47,135 --> 00:08:48,772
Mr Stafford Clark.

143
00:08:49,495 --> 00:08:52,694
Third, my lord,
as the result of point two,

144
00:08:52,695 --> 00:08:54,494
Mrs Hurst's 10-year-old daughter

145
00:08:54,495 --> 00:08:58,334
was sexually assaulted
by the adopted child.

146
00:08:58,335 --> 00:09:01,214
We would argue that the council
could have foreseen this,

147
00:09:01,215 --> 00:09:04,934
but did nothing to alert
Mrs Hurst and her husband.

148
00:09:04,935 --> 00:09:08,894
If that proves to be the case,
it could result in criminal proceedings.

149
00:09:08,895 --> 00:09:13,168
Mrs Hurst didn't lightly enter
into this action.

150
00:09:13,215 --> 00:09:17,934
Trying her utmost to settle
her adopted son has cost her marriage.

151
00:09:17,935 --> 00:09:19,295
Thank you.

152
00:09:25,815 --> 00:09:28,254
How far in is the schoolboy rape case?

153
00:09:28,255 --> 00:09:32,654
Oh, Mr Justice everard is hearing
some legal arguments now, Judge.

154
00:09:32,655 --> 00:09:36,814
Mrs Hurst took on a tough nut
when she took on that boy.

155
00:09:36,815 --> 00:09:39,894
It's surprising what some people do.

156
00:09:39,895 --> 00:09:43,374
My brother and sister-in-law
foster children.

157
00:09:43,375 --> 00:09:45,534
Some of them
have stolen money from them,

158
00:09:45,535 --> 00:09:49,172
one attacked them, but still they do it.

159
00:09:49,295 --> 00:09:51,854
It seems Mrs Hurst
can't give up on this child,

160
00:09:51,855 --> 00:09:53,574
in spite of all he's done.

161
00:09:53,575 --> 00:09:58,574
Well, I don't suppose she can,
not after losing her son.

162
00:09:58,575 --> 00:10:01,334
She crashed into a builder's truck,

163
00:10:01,335 --> 00:10:04,734
and a scaffolding pole
went through the windscreen

164
00:10:04,735 --> 00:10:06,495
and killed her son.

165
00:10:10,095 --> 00:10:12,014
Mrs Channing,
whilst I don't want to turn this

166
00:10:12,015 --> 00:10:14,174
pre-trial hearing into a trial,

167
00:10:14,175 --> 00:10:16,654
there are some questions
that I would like answered.

168
00:10:16,655 --> 00:10:18,334
Is there anyone here from the council?

169
00:10:18,335 --> 00:10:20,734
The case officer, Mrs Tredaway.

170
00:10:20,735 --> 00:10:22,917
Ah, yes. Would you mind?

171
00:10:24,695 --> 00:10:29,334
Mrs Tredaway, after Mr and Mrs Hurst
had established their suitability,

172
00:10:29,335 --> 00:10:31,894
how did you settle on Paul Lawrence?

173
00:10:31,895 --> 00:10:34,655
Through a series of meetings.

174
00:10:34,735 --> 00:10:36,454
Yes, that's clear from the file.

175
00:10:36,455 --> 00:10:39,654
What I mean is
how was Paul Lawrence chosen?

176
00:10:39,655 --> 00:10:43,054
Well, Mr and Mrs Hurst met
a number of children around Paul's age.

177
00:10:43,055 --> 00:10:44,854
They wanted an older child.

178
00:10:44,855 --> 00:10:46,934
And was Paul receptive to them?

179
00:10:46,935 --> 00:10:48,975
No. He was rude.

180
00:10:49,695 --> 00:10:51,854
Mr Hurst wanted to reject him.

181
00:10:51,855 --> 00:10:53,734
But he changed his mind?

182
00:10:53,735 --> 00:10:58,694
I think Mrs Hurst persuaded him to take
on the child least likely to be adopted.

183
00:10:58,695 --> 00:10:59,695
Paul.

184
00:11:00,375 --> 00:11:04,774
Did you paint
an overly optimistic view of this boy?

185
00:11:04,775 --> 00:11:06,594
We didn't paint any.

186
00:11:06,655 --> 00:11:09,974
His actions said everything.
He was hostile from day one.

187
00:11:09,975 --> 00:11:11,885
All right, thank you.

188
00:11:13,055 --> 00:11:14,374
Mr Stafford Clark?

189
00:11:14,375 --> 00:11:17,921
Yes, there are some questions, my lord.

190
00:11:18,015 --> 00:11:21,214
Mrs Tredaway,
how much information was given

191
00:11:21,215 --> 00:11:24,094
to Mrs Hurst and her former husband
about this boy?

192
00:11:24,095 --> 00:11:29,459
It's difficult to be precise.
Mostly, it was given verbally.

193
00:11:29,575 --> 00:11:32,725
Did anyone minute the meetings?

194
00:11:32,815 --> 00:11:35,214
That doesn't usually happen.

195
00:11:35,215 --> 00:11:38,014
Even though parents are at their most

196
00:11:38,015 --> 00:11:41,334
emotionally vulnerable
on such occasions,

197
00:11:41,335 --> 00:11:44,254
you saw no reason
to keep a written note?

198
00:11:44,255 --> 00:11:46,801
No. We don't do it that way.

199
00:11:46,815 --> 00:11:50,014
So when you indicate
you fully informed Mrs Hurst

200
00:11:50,015 --> 00:11:53,295
as to the dangers this boy posed,

201
00:11:53,375 --> 00:11:56,739
we only have your word, Mrs Tredaway.

202
00:12:01,255 --> 00:12:02,325
Thank you.

203
00:12:02,415 --> 00:12:03,855
Mrs Channing.

204
00:12:05,575 --> 00:12:08,974
How long have you worked
in adoption, Mrs Tredaway?

205
00:12:08,975 --> 00:12:10,735
Eight years.

206
00:12:10,815 --> 00:12:13,854
How many adoptions
have you supervised in that time?

207
00:12:13,855 --> 00:12:15,335
About 300.

208
00:12:16,335 --> 00:12:19,414
Before Mr and Mrs Hurst adopted Paul,

209
00:12:19,415 --> 00:12:23,814
were you certain
they knew what they were taking on?

210
00:12:23,815 --> 00:12:26,634
Mrs Hurst kept pushing for him.

211
00:12:27,415 --> 00:12:30,574
It was obvious Paul was very vulnerable,

212
00:12:30,575 --> 00:12:36,374
but having begun the process of choosing
him, we felt we couldn't abandon him.

213
00:12:36,375 --> 00:12:41,014
The choosing process is horrible.
A sea of appealing faces.

214
00:12:41,015 --> 00:12:43,974
Were you warned by the council
that he might be difficult?

215
00:12:43,975 --> 00:12:45,455
Yes.

216
00:12:46,335 --> 00:12:48,974
We felt they almost
didn't want to place him.

217
00:12:48,975 --> 00:12:50,894
They seemed to be punishing him.

218
00:12:50,895 --> 00:12:52,854
What was your husband's view?

219
00:12:52,855 --> 00:12:56,774
He thought the council
was behaving shamefully.

220
00:12:56,775 --> 00:13:00,054
It was only after Paul came
to live with us that he had doubts.

221
00:13:00,055 --> 00:13:01,414
Before the adoption?

222
00:13:01,415 --> 00:13:06,414
Towards the end of the trial period.
He thought Paul was too disruptive

223
00:13:06,415 --> 00:13:08,894
and he needed all our attention.

224
00:13:08,895 --> 00:13:11,986
Why did you choose him, Mrs Hurst?

225
00:13:12,935 --> 00:13:16,026
I've asked myself that many times.

226
00:13:16,535 --> 00:13:19,774
I thought he deserved a second chance.

227
00:13:19,775 --> 00:13:24,685
Well, no, not a second chance.
He'd never had a chance.

228
00:13:27,975 --> 00:13:29,612
Mr Stafford Clark.

229
00:13:31,455 --> 00:13:32,525
Mrs Hurst,

230
00:13:33,615 --> 00:13:37,654
at what point
did you invest emotionally in Paul?

231
00:13:37,655 --> 00:13:42,414
I'd met him about three times
when I knew he was the one.

232
00:13:42,415 --> 00:13:44,694
I felt strangely drawn to him.

233
00:13:44,695 --> 00:13:49,414
At any point in your approach to Paul
or during the trial period,

234
00:13:49,415 --> 00:13:54,414
did anyone from the council tell you
he posed a danger to your family?

235
00:13:54,415 --> 00:13:56,135
No, no one did.

236
00:13:57,655 --> 00:14:00,694
Isn't it true, Mrs Hurst, you were
warned again and again about the boy,

237
00:14:00,695 --> 00:14:02,494
but went ahead anyway?

238
00:14:02,495 --> 00:14:05,894
People were negative
and hurtful about Paul.

239
00:14:05,895 --> 00:14:09,214
Never once did they say
he was dangerous.

240
00:14:09,215 --> 00:14:13,654
Didn't Mrs Tredaway say to you
on your fifth visit with Paul,

241
00:14:13,655 --> 00:14:19,383
and I quote, "He could cause
your family a great deal of grief"?

242
00:14:20,055 --> 00:14:22,874
I don't recall her saying that.

243
00:14:23,335 --> 00:14:25,254
Perhaps you didn't want
to hear the warnings.

244
00:14:25,255 --> 00:14:28,801
I wanted to give Paul a chance in life.

245
00:14:28,815 --> 00:14:32,634
They never seemed
to want him to have that.

246
00:14:33,495 --> 00:14:36,677
This is painful for you, Mrs Hurst,

247
00:14:36,735 --> 00:14:42,554
but it's likely to get more painful
if it goes to a full hearing.

248
00:14:42,655 --> 00:14:45,454
Mr Stafford Clark,
I am going to allow the claimant

249
00:14:45,455 --> 00:14:47,894
limited access to the council's database

250
00:14:47,895 --> 00:14:50,654
and I will have this matter back
here in one week.

251
00:14:50,655 --> 00:14:51,725
All rise.

252
00:14:53,735 --> 00:14:55,554
BOY: Go, tackle him.

253
00:14:55,855 --> 00:14:57,094
(BOYS CHATTeRING)

254
00:14:57,095 --> 00:15:01,534
What makes a woman want to take on
the likes of Paul Lawrence?

255
00:15:01,535 --> 00:15:03,054
Isn't the argument about whether

256
00:15:03,055 --> 00:15:06,534
the report gave a more
optimistic picture than was true?

257
00:15:06,535 --> 00:15:09,534
The picture the council painted
wasn't that optimistic.

258
00:15:09,535 --> 00:15:10,765
Mmm.

259
00:15:10,855 --> 00:15:14,454
It could be that Vivienne Hurst
feels guilty about the loss of her son.

260
00:15:14,455 --> 00:15:18,014
She may have picked the most difficult
child to adopt to punish herself.

261
00:15:18,015 --> 00:15:21,174
She punished the whole family then.

262
00:15:21,175 --> 00:15:24,894
JO: Perhaps subconsciously
she wanted to destroy them all.

263
00:15:24,895 --> 00:15:28,534
- It's a very dangerous course to take.
- Mmm.

264
00:15:28,535 --> 00:15:31,535
(BOYS GROANING IN DISAPPOINTMeNT)

265
00:15:33,575 --> 00:15:34,855
(LAUGHS)

266
00:15:38,375 --> 00:15:40,921
How's your sabbatical going?

267
00:15:41,175 --> 00:15:43,448
I'm taking on Alan Ferns.

268
00:15:44,535 --> 00:15:48,354
You do know
that I'm hearing it, don't you?

269
00:15:48,535 --> 00:15:51,445
I can live with that if you can.

270
00:15:52,055 --> 00:15:57,601
Won't taking on more work
prejudice your chances with Michael?

271
00:15:58,215 --> 00:15:59,215
- Yes!
- Ah!

272
00:15:59,295 --> 00:16:00,374
(WHISTLe BLOWING)

273
00:16:00,375 --> 00:16:01,654
(BOYS CHeeRING)

274
00:16:01,655 --> 00:16:03,645
Well done! Good shot!

275
00:16:06,015 --> 00:16:10,334
Jo, the CPS has asked me to make
Alan Ferns an offer,

276
00:16:10,335 --> 00:16:12,374
against my advice, I have to say.

277
00:16:12,375 --> 00:16:15,214
They'll accept a plea
of guilty to manslaughter.

278
00:16:15,215 --> 00:16:17,334
Has the CCTV footage faded?

279
00:16:17,335 --> 00:16:20,972
No, nor has the jury's dislike
of nonces.

280
00:16:21,615 --> 00:16:23,615
I'll see what he says.

281
00:16:25,775 --> 00:16:28,974
Manslaughter should mean
another 15 years in prison.

282
00:16:28,975 --> 00:16:32,574
As your lawyer, I have to advise you
this is a good offer.

283
00:16:32,575 --> 00:16:35,054
Well, you know what public feeling is.

284
00:16:35,055 --> 00:16:37,614
THe Sun called Baylis a monster.

285
00:16:37,615 --> 00:16:39,094
So I think I'll take my chances

286
00:16:39,095 --> 00:16:42,534
with the ordinary men and women
on the jury, Mrs Mills.

287
00:16:42,535 --> 00:16:43,535
Thank you.

288
00:16:44,295 --> 00:16:45,841
Stand up, please.

289
00:16:46,815 --> 00:16:49,495
Alan Peter Ferns,

290
00:16:49,575 --> 00:16:53,615
to the charge of the murder
of Barry Baylis,

291
00:16:53,695 --> 00:16:56,934
how do you plead, guilty or not guilty?

292
00:16:56,935 --> 00:16:58,055
Not guilty.

293
00:16:58,695 --> 00:17:00,015
Sit down.

294
00:17:02,695 --> 00:17:05,814
What exactly is your defence, Mrs Mills?

295
00:17:05,815 --> 00:17:09,694
Prevention of crime, my lord,
a pre-emptive strike.

296
00:17:09,695 --> 00:17:11,335
I don't see how.

297
00:17:12,215 --> 00:17:17,694
The victim was in prison. I believe
there is CCTV footage of the entire act.

298
00:17:17,695 --> 00:17:21,614
Perhaps I should make the court aware
that the Crown has indicated

299
00:17:21,615 --> 00:17:24,654
that it might accept a plea
to a lesser alternative.

300
00:17:24,655 --> 00:17:27,292
Which my client won't accept.

301
00:17:27,615 --> 00:17:31,574
- You pointed out the advantages?
- He sees only disadvantages.

302
00:17:31,575 --> 00:17:32,645
Thank you.

303
00:17:33,775 --> 00:17:35,694
You realise that you risk
a life sentence

304
00:17:35,695 --> 00:17:37,894
if you're found guilty of murder,
Mr Ferns?

305
00:17:37,895 --> 00:17:39,259
Yes, I do, sir.

306
00:17:40,335 --> 00:17:42,894
If you run this,
I am going to have to tell the jury

307
00:17:42,895 --> 00:17:44,934
what the limitations
of such a defence are.

308
00:17:44,935 --> 00:17:48,294
Everyone has the right
to a defence, my lord.

309
00:17:48,295 --> 00:17:49,295
Thank you.

310
00:17:49,375 --> 00:17:51,534
Why don't you try
diminished responsibility?

311
00:17:51,535 --> 00:17:54,445
Mr Ferns knew what he was doing.

312
00:17:54,775 --> 00:17:59,774
Prevention of a crime
can only apply if the victim, Baylis,

313
00:17:59,775 --> 00:18:06,685
was threatening to kill or seriously
injure someone and that threat was real.

314
00:18:08,575 --> 00:18:11,094
That's the defence we're running.

315
00:18:11,095 --> 00:18:13,550
Fine. Let's swear the jury.

316
00:18:15,695 --> 00:18:17,734
Alan Ferns, the defendant,

317
00:18:17,735 --> 00:18:21,374
killed Barry Baylis,
a security executive.

318
00:18:21,375 --> 00:18:25,174
This, ladies and gentlemen,
is an indisputable fact.

319
00:18:25,175 --> 00:18:28,614
The whole act was captured
on a CCTV camera

320
00:18:28,615 --> 00:18:33,614
in the prison where the deceased man
was serving a three-month sentence.

321
00:18:33,615 --> 00:18:36,161
The prosecution's contention

322
00:18:36,175 --> 00:18:39,934
is that the defendant, Alan Ferns,
planned this killing

323
00:18:39,935 --> 00:18:43,645
in the most cold
and calculating of ways.

324
00:18:44,655 --> 00:18:48,414
He didn't attack Baylis
in the heat of the moment.

325
00:18:48,415 --> 00:18:51,052
It was entirely premeditated.

326
00:18:52,615 --> 00:18:54,254
With your leave, my lord,

327
00:18:54,255 --> 00:18:56,174
I'd like to make
an opening address to the jury

328
00:18:56,175 --> 00:18:58,654
to explain the issues at the outset.

329
00:18:58,655 --> 00:19:05,746
This is not usual, Mrs Mills, but I'll
allow it in these unusual circumstances.

330
00:19:07,615 --> 00:19:09,414
At the time of the killing,

331
00:19:09,415 --> 00:19:13,614
Mr Ferns was at the end of a
seven-year prison sentence for assault.

332
00:19:13,615 --> 00:19:17,774
He'd taken the law into his own hands
and attacked a paedophile.

333
00:19:17,775 --> 00:19:21,094
This was a crime for which
Mr Ferns freely admitted guilt,

334
00:19:21,095 --> 00:19:22,734
though he'd shown no remorse,

335
00:19:22,735 --> 00:19:27,214
which was why he served
all of the seven years of his sentence.

336
00:19:27,215 --> 00:19:30,174
Mr Ferns only had one day left to serve.

337
00:19:30,175 --> 00:19:32,630
One day before his release.

338
00:19:33,615 --> 00:19:35,494
I'm telling you this,
ladies and gentlemen,

339
00:19:35,495 --> 00:19:37,974
because I want you to know
what was at stake for Mr Ferns

340
00:19:37,975 --> 00:19:41,612
when he felt
compelled to kill Mr Baylis.

341
00:19:42,575 --> 00:19:48,174
What is at issue here is whether or not
he was justified in doing this.

342
00:19:48,175 --> 00:19:52,214
Mr Baylis had been given
only a three-month prison sentence

343
00:19:52,215 --> 00:19:55,614
because he failed to produce
the hard drive of his computer

344
00:19:55,615 --> 00:20:00,094
during his trial for sexual abuse
of a nine-year-old.

345
00:20:00,095 --> 00:20:04,854
In the past five years, Baylis had been
arrested no less than eight times

346
00:20:04,855 --> 00:20:07,094
for offences against children.

347
00:20:07,095 --> 00:20:11,005
On each occasion,
he had evaded prosecution.

348
00:20:11,295 --> 00:20:14,134
Nothing, it seemed, would stop Mr Baylis

349
00:20:14,135 --> 00:20:17,614
in his pathological pursuit
of young children.

350
00:20:17,615 --> 00:20:20,615
Not the police, the courts,

351
00:20:20,695 --> 00:20:25,294
nor the ludicrously short
prison sentence he had received.

352
00:20:25,295 --> 00:20:27,477
Mr Ferns acted as he did

353
00:20:28,735 --> 00:20:32,008
to prevent others being preyed upon.

354
00:20:32,015 --> 00:20:33,015
Come on in.

355
00:20:33,095 --> 00:20:35,135
(CeLLO PLAYING)

356
00:20:40,335 --> 00:20:42,854
You've got some news for me
about Michael's alleged father?

357
00:20:42,855 --> 00:20:43,975
Yeah.

358
00:20:45,735 --> 00:20:48,295
His DNA test was positive.

359
00:20:51,175 --> 00:20:53,974
And now I'm supposed
to bow out of Michael's life?

360
00:20:53,975 --> 00:20:57,174
Well, Michael should meet him first.

361
00:20:57,175 --> 00:20:58,454
How long is he here?

362
00:20:58,455 --> 00:21:00,894
I think that depends
on how Michael responds to him.

363
00:21:00,895 --> 00:21:05,350
- If he doesn't like him?
- I suspect he'll leave.

364
00:21:06,335 --> 00:21:08,134
Does he have a family?

365
00:21:08,135 --> 00:21:12,174
Two teenage sons.
They live with him in Cape Town.

366
00:21:12,175 --> 00:21:17,903
He's called Marc Thompson, 46,
divorced, cardiovascular surgeon.

367
00:21:18,975 --> 00:21:21,614
Would you like me
to broach the subject with Michael?

368
00:21:21,615 --> 00:21:26,525
I'd like to meet Mr Thompson first.
Give him my number.

369
00:21:28,095 --> 00:21:30,534
How's it looking for Paul Lawrence?

370
00:21:30,535 --> 00:21:31,855
Not good.

371
00:21:33,255 --> 00:21:35,134
His adoptive mother claims

372
00:21:35,135 --> 00:21:38,374
that the council
failed to disclose his full history.

373
00:21:38,375 --> 00:21:42,734
Well, they're arguing
that the teacher consented.

374
00:21:42,735 --> 00:21:44,735
I don't see it myself.

375
00:21:45,775 --> 00:21:47,814
Mrs Hurst given evidence?

376
00:21:47,815 --> 00:21:49,894
You know, she's in court every day.

377
00:21:49,895 --> 00:21:54,574
You know, I really don't see
why anyone would take this boy.

378
00:21:54,575 --> 00:21:58,303
He's 13, John,
he scares me half to death.

379
00:21:58,655 --> 00:22:02,414
- Would you mind if I sat in?
- No, by all means.

380
00:22:02,415 --> 00:22:04,174
Your legal position is not good.

381
00:22:04,175 --> 00:22:06,294
Michael's father
could fight you for custody.

382
00:22:06,295 --> 00:22:08,534
The Judge is going to be delayed
by about half an hour.

383
00:22:08,535 --> 00:22:12,054
- I'll come and find you when he's ready.
- Thank you.

384
00:22:12,055 --> 00:22:14,015
- Tea?
- Yeah, go on.

385
00:22:17,855 --> 00:22:19,494
Paul Lawrence
had been in and out of care

386
00:22:19,495 --> 00:22:22,094
since his grandmother's death
when he was three years old.

387
00:22:22,095 --> 00:22:24,414
How did Paul's grandmother die?

388
00:22:24,415 --> 00:22:27,574
She was murdered by another drug addict
who tried to steal her drugs.

389
00:22:27,575 --> 00:22:30,134
What effect do you think
this might have had on Paul?

390
00:22:30,135 --> 00:22:33,894
My lord, this witness
is not qualified to answer that.

391
00:22:33,895 --> 00:22:37,134
I'd like to hear his answer
all the same.

392
00:22:37,135 --> 00:22:40,574
I'd say that was probably the start of
all his violent anti-social behaviour.

393
00:22:40,575 --> 00:22:45,014
What evidence is there
of this anti-social behaviour?

394
00:22:45,015 --> 00:22:47,925
He was uncontrollable at school.

395
00:22:48,055 --> 00:22:51,174
He displayed sexually predatory
behaviour both there

396
00:22:51,175 --> 00:22:53,654
and to female members
of foster parents' families.

397
00:22:53,655 --> 00:22:56,054
There's no reference to this
in the council's reports.

398
00:22:56,055 --> 00:22:59,054
I certainly told colleagues about him.

399
00:22:59,055 --> 00:23:03,854
If this boy was as dangerous
as you say, why would anyone adopt him?

400
00:23:03,855 --> 00:23:07,492
I think his parents were terribly brave.

401
00:23:08,495 --> 00:23:11,814
- I'm looking for Mrs Mills.
- She's there.

402
00:23:11,815 --> 00:23:13,374
All right. Thanks.

403
00:23:13,375 --> 00:23:16,194
They haven't gone awry yet, Jo.

404
00:23:16,615 --> 00:23:19,894
Sitting as a circuit judge
would stabilise your life.

405
00:23:19,895 --> 00:23:21,694
I'm not saying it would help much.

406
00:23:21,695 --> 00:23:25,174
Excuse me. Mrs Mills? I'm Marc Thompson.

407
00:23:25,175 --> 00:23:28,414
Em, Mrs Tredaway from the council
said I should contact you.

408
00:23:28,415 --> 00:23:30,134
I did telephone your clerk.

409
00:23:30,135 --> 00:23:35,499
Who told you I'm in the middle
of a murder trial. Excuse me.

410
00:23:37,695 --> 00:23:39,495
(PeOPLe CHATTeRING)

411
00:23:43,375 --> 00:23:46,414
Jo, it's not for me to interfere...

412
00:23:46,415 --> 00:23:50,254
- He's Michael's father.
- I guessed as much.

413
00:23:50,255 --> 00:23:53,801
How long will you be able to avoid him?

414
00:23:54,815 --> 00:23:57,179
I know, it's stupid of me.

415
00:24:02,775 --> 00:24:04,255
Mr Thompson?

416
00:24:06,775 --> 00:24:09,854
I must apologise for my behaviour.
I'm sorry.

417
00:24:09,855 --> 00:24:11,814
Well, it must have been a big shock.

418
00:24:11,815 --> 00:24:12,815
Well...

419
00:24:14,175 --> 00:24:16,721
Would you like a cup of tea?

420
00:24:17,135 --> 00:24:18,734
If it won't make you late.

421
00:24:18,735 --> 00:24:20,826
We haven't started yet.

422
00:24:26,255 --> 00:24:28,494
Michael's doing very well at school.

423
00:24:28,495 --> 00:24:32,950
Yes, the social services told me
he's very bright.

424
00:24:35,775 --> 00:24:40,503
- I've grown very fond of him.
- Yes, I can see that.

425
00:24:40,575 --> 00:24:42,694
Perhaps I should leave well alone.

426
00:24:42,695 --> 00:24:45,786
You're his father. He has to know.

427
00:24:48,895 --> 00:24:51,294
His mother made no attempt
to contact me.

428
00:24:51,295 --> 00:24:54,454
- Did she know where you were?
- I wasn't hard to find.

429
00:24:54,455 --> 00:24:57,455
Perhaps because you were married?

430
00:25:01,615 --> 00:25:04,525
She and Michael were very close.

431
00:25:08,015 --> 00:25:11,054
Do you think that my being here
will upset him?

432
00:25:11,055 --> 00:25:12,894
When you've lost the person
who was the centre of your life,

433
00:25:12,895 --> 00:25:14,805
breathing upsets you.

434
00:25:16,175 --> 00:25:20,094
What we have to assess is what's
in Michael's best long-term interests.

435
00:25:20,095 --> 00:25:23,005
Have you reached any conclusion?

436
00:25:25,575 --> 00:25:29,030
I think he ought to at least meet you.

437
00:25:30,175 --> 00:25:33,014
Mr Sweeney, during
the course of your duties,

438
00:25:33,015 --> 00:25:35,734
had you been able
to get close to Alan Ferns?

439
00:25:35,735 --> 00:25:37,694
Yes. He was quite friendly.

440
00:25:37,695 --> 00:25:38,934
Why didn't you come and get me?

441
00:25:38,935 --> 00:25:43,299
Clive did.
He said you were deep in conversation.

442
00:25:43,815 --> 00:25:46,134
What was the defendant's state of mind

443
00:25:46,135 --> 00:25:48,734
prior to the death of Mr Baylis?

444
00:25:48,735 --> 00:25:51,015
Perfectly calm. Lucid.

445
00:25:51,095 --> 00:25:54,494
We'd been talking about
his second degree he'd just got.

446
00:25:54,495 --> 00:25:57,415
Was he agitated or emotional?

447
00:25:57,495 --> 00:26:01,768
No. He was calm,
looking forward to his release.

448
00:26:02,215 --> 00:26:04,215
Thank you, Mr Sweeney.

449
00:26:08,935 --> 00:26:11,374
Did he talk about
what he'd do after his release?

450
00:26:11,375 --> 00:26:14,334
He was going to work
for the charity he'd started.

451
00:26:14,335 --> 00:26:17,414
Did you know what Mr Ferns
was serving his sentence for?

452
00:26:17,415 --> 00:26:19,254
Beating up a paedophile.

453
00:26:19,255 --> 00:26:22,614
Was it sensible
letting a paedophile near Mr Ferns?

454
00:26:22,615 --> 00:26:26,734
My lord, the allegations against the
victim in this case were never proven.

455
00:26:26,735 --> 00:26:31,334
For all we know, Mr Baylis was never
successfully prosecuted for child abuse

456
00:26:31,335 --> 00:26:33,414
because he was wholly innocent.

457
00:26:33,415 --> 00:26:36,961
Remind us what Mr Baylis' sentence was.

458
00:26:37,095 --> 00:26:38,095
Er...

459
00:26:39,095 --> 00:26:40,414
Three months, my lord.

460
00:26:40,415 --> 00:26:42,734
- Thank you.
- For perverting the course of justice.

461
00:26:42,735 --> 00:26:46,008
The main case against him collapsed.

462
00:26:46,935 --> 00:26:52,014
Mrs Mills is free to speculate on
whether or not Baylis was a paedophile.

463
00:26:52,015 --> 00:26:57,534
But since he was never convicted,
it remains only speculation.

464
00:26:57,535 --> 00:26:58,605
Mrs Mills.

465
00:27:00,215 --> 00:27:03,654
Did Mr Ferns comment
on the puny sentence Baylis received?

466
00:27:03,655 --> 00:27:06,094
He was outraged. We all were.

467
00:27:06,095 --> 00:27:08,974
Did the defendant make any threats
about Mr Baylis?

468
00:27:08,975 --> 00:27:11,134
He said someone ought to top him.

469
00:27:11,135 --> 00:27:13,694
Did he seem fearful
of Baylis getting out of prison?

470
00:27:13,695 --> 00:27:15,854
He was pretty wound up about it.

471
00:27:15,855 --> 00:27:20,414
He'd got a lot of information
about what Baylis had done to children.

472
00:27:20,415 --> 00:27:23,174
Did he ever speak
about his own childhood?

473
00:27:23,175 --> 00:27:24,535
Not really.

474
00:27:25,175 --> 00:27:30,630
Word was he'd been sexually abused
by his mother's boyfriend.

475
00:27:30,815 --> 00:27:33,974
Were you surprised at Mr Ferns
killing Mr Baylis?

476
00:27:33,975 --> 00:27:35,294
I can't say I was.

477
00:27:35,295 --> 00:27:37,454
Despite it being
the day before his release?

478
00:27:37,455 --> 00:27:39,974
He believed Baylis and his kind
had to be stopped.

479
00:27:39,975 --> 00:27:42,574
Stopped from abusing children.

480
00:27:42,575 --> 00:27:43,734
NORWALK: My lord.

481
00:27:43,735 --> 00:27:45,099
(ALL MURMURING)

482
00:27:45,135 --> 00:27:48,294
Mrs Mills, would you care
to rephrase your statement?

483
00:27:48,295 --> 00:27:50,659
No, I don't think I would.

484
00:27:56,495 --> 00:28:00,614
I would like you to disregard
defence counsel's last statement.

485
00:28:00,615 --> 00:28:04,094
It is incumbent upon counsel
to ask questions,

486
00:28:04,095 --> 00:28:05,914
not make statements.

487
00:28:12,695 --> 00:28:16,695
Do you ever think
about your father, Michael?

488
00:28:17,215 --> 00:28:21,306
Sometimes, when the other dads
come to school.

489
00:28:24,215 --> 00:28:26,334
Do you wonder what he's like?

490
00:28:26,335 --> 00:28:28,335
I don't have a father.

491
00:28:33,735 --> 00:28:35,565
I met him today.

492
00:28:35,655 --> 00:28:37,019
He's very nice.

493
00:28:38,295 --> 00:28:42,494
He's been living in South Africa.
He didn't know about you.

494
00:28:42,495 --> 00:28:44,885
Mum said I don't.

495
00:28:45,615 --> 00:28:51,434
Well, perhaps she didn't tell you
because she wasn't sure of him.

496
00:28:53,375 --> 00:28:56,103
I've got to do cello practice.

497
00:28:57,815 --> 00:28:59,255
(DOOR SLAMS)

498
00:29:00,135 --> 00:29:04,975
(CeLLO PLAYING)

499
00:29:08,055 --> 00:29:09,965
Michael, can we talk?

500
00:29:11,775 --> 00:29:14,174
- I have to practise.
- You can practise later.

501
00:29:14,175 --> 00:29:16,448
I have to do my homework.

502
00:29:22,695 --> 00:29:26,814
Sweetheart, sometimes grown-ups
don't tell children things

503
00:29:26,815 --> 00:29:29,294
because they want to protect them.

504
00:29:29,295 --> 00:29:32,023
Why didn't he come and get me?

505
00:29:32,055 --> 00:29:34,614
He didn't know
anything about you till now.

506
00:29:34,615 --> 00:29:36,888
Do I have to go with him?

507
00:29:36,935 --> 00:29:38,694
Would you like to meet him?

508
00:29:38,695 --> 00:29:41,694
- No. I don't like him.
- On what evidence?

509
00:29:41,695 --> 00:29:44,374
If you're going to be a barrister, you
have to learn to assess the evidence.

510
00:29:44,375 --> 00:29:47,194
I don't want to be a barrister.

511
00:29:47,255 --> 00:29:48,975
I don't blame you.

512
00:30:06,375 --> 00:30:07,830
What's his name?

513
00:30:08,935 --> 00:30:10,299
Is it curiosity

514
00:30:11,175 --> 00:30:13,994
or are you assembling evidence?

515
00:30:17,015 --> 00:30:19,214
It's Marc Thompson. He's a surgeon.

516
00:30:19,215 --> 00:30:23,174
- But I'm not called Thompson.
- No, you've got your mum's name.

517
00:30:23,175 --> 00:30:24,903
He seems very nice.

518
00:30:25,295 --> 00:30:26,814
Will you marry him?

519
00:30:26,815 --> 00:30:28,015
(LAUGHS)

520
00:30:28,095 --> 00:30:32,914
That might solve problems for you.
It wouldn't for me.

521
00:30:33,535 --> 00:30:35,990
Because you love the Judge?

522
00:30:36,535 --> 00:30:37,765
Maybe.

523
00:30:39,695 --> 00:30:43,332
We could meet your dad
somewhere for tea.

524
00:30:43,495 --> 00:30:45,974
Sergeant Martin, were you the detective

525
00:30:45,975 --> 00:30:49,774
who led the investigation
into the murder of Mr Baylis?

526
00:30:49,775 --> 00:30:52,694
Yes. It was an open-and-shut case.

527
00:30:52,695 --> 00:30:55,494
The whole thing was acted out
on a CCTV camera.

528
00:30:55,495 --> 00:30:57,859
Can we see that, Mr Usher?

529
00:30:59,175 --> 00:31:04,448
Some of you may find this disturbing,
ladies and gentlemen.

530
00:31:10,975 --> 00:31:12,775
(JURORS MURMURING)

531
00:31:33,495 --> 00:31:38,214
Is this the tape that was removed
from the camera following the killing?

532
00:31:38,215 --> 00:31:40,685
- Yes, it is.
- Thank you.

533
00:31:43,455 --> 00:31:45,974
Was the victim known
to the police, Sergeant?

534
00:31:45,975 --> 00:31:49,214
He had been under investigation
a number of times.

535
00:31:49,215 --> 00:31:53,014
Isn't it true that he'd been in court
no less than eight times

536
00:31:53,015 --> 00:31:56,854
- for indecent assaults on children?
- Yes, he was.

537
00:31:56,855 --> 00:32:00,174
- My lord...
- each time he managed to escape justice.

538
00:32:00,175 --> 00:32:01,294
Really, my lord.

539
00:32:01,295 --> 00:32:04,294
Yes, all right.
Mrs Mills, this is not a proper line.

540
00:32:04,295 --> 00:32:06,734
The victim of Mr Ferns' attack
is not on trial.

541
00:32:06,735 --> 00:32:09,174
No, my lord, that's the whole point.
He should have been.

542
00:32:09,175 --> 00:32:11,734
If it wasn't for his escaping justice,
my client wouldn't be here.

543
00:32:11,735 --> 00:32:13,495
Mrs Mills, enough.

544
00:32:14,655 --> 00:32:18,383
Now, I take it
you have no more questions.

545
00:32:18,455 --> 00:32:19,655
No, my lord.

546
00:32:20,455 --> 00:32:22,254
Mr Norwalk, do you want to come back?

547
00:32:22,255 --> 00:32:24,294
No. Thank you, my lord.

548
00:32:24,295 --> 00:32:26,054
Thank you, Detective Sergeant.
You can step down.

549
00:32:26,055 --> 00:32:27,125
Thank you.

550
00:32:29,655 --> 00:32:33,454
The Crown would like to call
Dr Margot Lupton.

551
00:32:33,455 --> 00:32:37,094
Dr Lupton is a clinical psychologist
who examined the defendant

552
00:32:37,095 --> 00:32:41,565
prior to and subsequent to
his killing Mr Baylis.

553
00:32:42,295 --> 00:32:46,054
Over what period did you examine
the defendant?

554
00:32:46,055 --> 00:32:47,894
On and off over four years.

555
00:32:47,895 --> 00:32:50,854
And did you reach any conclusions?

556
00:32:50,855 --> 00:32:57,583
Mr Ferns is intelligent, articulate,
capable of grasping abstract concepts.

557
00:32:58,095 --> 00:33:01,774
He's highly opinionated,
somewhat bigoted,

558
00:33:01,775 --> 00:33:04,894
has a strong need to always be right.

559
00:33:04,895 --> 00:33:08,014
And did you describe him
in one of your reports

560
00:33:08,015 --> 00:33:11,805
as having a pathological need
for revenge?

561
00:33:12,215 --> 00:33:14,174
Yes, I did observe that.

562
00:33:14,175 --> 00:33:17,614
And what was the basis
of this observation?

563
00:33:17,615 --> 00:33:21,254
He was serially sexually abused
as a child and as a result

564
00:33:21,255 --> 00:33:25,294
feels he has an absolute right
to inflict punishment on paedophiles.

565
00:33:25,295 --> 00:33:29,694
And would this justification
have extended to Barry Baylis?

566
00:33:29,695 --> 00:33:32,494
Mr Baylis would have fitted entirely
the model

567
00:33:32,495 --> 00:33:35,654
Alan Ferns needed to avenge himself on.

568
00:33:35,655 --> 00:33:39,294
Even though he had not been
convicted of child abuse?

569
00:33:39,295 --> 00:33:42,614
Well, that in a sense made him
a more suitable target.

570
00:33:42,615 --> 00:33:48,797
In Mr Ferns' eyes, the law had failed
to punish Baylis, so he had to.

571
00:33:49,815 --> 00:33:51,135
Thank you.

572
00:33:55,015 --> 00:33:57,197
You don't like Mr Ferns.

573
00:33:57,735 --> 00:34:00,014
I don't think that's true.

574
00:34:00,015 --> 00:34:03,294
Well, then look at item 37
in your bundle,

575
00:34:03,295 --> 00:34:07,568
your report on Mr Ferns
over a four-year period.

576
00:34:09,535 --> 00:34:13,734
"An arrogant egoist
who always has to have the last word."

577
00:34:13,735 --> 00:34:16,281
It's true. That's how he is.

578
00:34:16,455 --> 00:34:20,974
"Always using his intellect
to belittle me in group sessions."

579
00:34:20,975 --> 00:34:22,430
He does do that.

580
00:34:23,015 --> 00:34:24,614
Isn't it true that Alan Ferns' intellect

581
00:34:24,615 --> 00:34:27,934
put him and you
on a dangerous collision course?

582
00:34:27,935 --> 00:34:31,854
I think he uses his intellect
to mask the pain he's feeling.

583
00:34:31,855 --> 00:34:34,374
Did he ever talk about the pain
he was feeling?

584
00:34:34,375 --> 00:34:37,014
Not really. It was too deeply buried.

585
00:34:37,015 --> 00:34:40,214
Could it be that he had worked
through that pain and overcome it?

586
00:34:40,215 --> 00:34:42,125
I don't think he did.

587
00:34:42,455 --> 00:34:46,214
The pain merely manifested itself
in fantasy.

588
00:34:46,215 --> 00:34:48,854
He would act out those fantasies

589
00:34:48,855 --> 00:34:53,574
through his Mr Right personality,
correcting injustices in the world.

590
00:34:53,575 --> 00:34:57,254
Killing Mr Baylis
was part of that fantasy.

591
00:34:57,255 --> 00:35:00,095
He did so with premeditation

592
00:35:00,175 --> 00:35:04,094
in the clear belief
that he was right to do so.

593
00:35:04,095 --> 00:35:06,974
This is a man who,
despite his background,

594
00:35:06,975 --> 00:35:08,494
managed to study and get

595
00:35:08,495 --> 00:35:11,334
two first-class law degrees
whilst in prison.

596
00:35:11,335 --> 00:35:13,974
Is that indicative of someone
who hasn't overcome

597
00:35:13,975 --> 00:35:17,374
the incapacitating traumas
of his childhood?

598
00:35:17,375 --> 00:35:20,654
It is my belief
that he'd overcome little

599
00:35:20,655 --> 00:35:24,614
because he still harbours
vengeful feelings towards paedophiles.

600
00:35:24,615 --> 00:35:29,694
Could he not have been so overwhelmed by
a sense of injustice that he had to act

601
00:35:29,695 --> 00:35:34,494
because he thought the man so dangerous
he had to be stopped?

602
00:35:34,495 --> 00:35:37,859
That is a delusion he operated under.

603
00:35:38,015 --> 00:35:39,415
"I am right."

604
00:35:39,855 --> 00:35:43,574
Isn't that what you're doing
with your opinion?

605
00:35:43,575 --> 00:35:47,174
Mrs Mills,
Dr Lupton is an expert witness.

606
00:35:47,175 --> 00:35:50,694
Her observations are given based
on her qualifications and experience.

607
00:35:50,695 --> 00:35:53,574
Yes, formed as a result
of a personality clash

608
00:35:53,575 --> 00:35:56,848
where she wanted to appear superior.

609
00:35:58,895 --> 00:36:00,814
Mrs Mills, the Judge wants a word.

610
00:36:00,815 --> 00:36:03,174
- Me too, Mrs Cooper?
- No, just Mrs Mills.

611
00:36:03,175 --> 00:36:05,214
I can't. I'm rushing to meet
Michael's father with Michael.

612
00:36:05,215 --> 00:36:07,670
I can't be late. I'm sorry.

613
00:36:10,455 --> 00:36:12,001
Michael's father?

614
00:36:12,735 --> 00:36:14,934
Is he the dark-haired man
who was with her in court?

615
00:36:14,935 --> 00:36:16,694
I didn't notice him, Judge.

616
00:36:16,695 --> 00:36:19,332
No, he's not very noticeable.

617
00:36:20,535 --> 00:36:23,534
Do you want Mrs Hurst back tomorrow?

618
00:36:23,535 --> 00:36:27,899
Yes, please.
Would you tell the parties involved?

619
00:36:33,775 --> 00:36:36,334
He's got two children.
They live in Cape Town.

620
00:36:36,335 --> 00:36:37,695
How nice.

621
00:36:37,775 --> 00:36:41,174
Now I don't know much about Cape Town,
but he seems terribly nice.

622
00:36:41,175 --> 00:36:44,774
- What if he doesn't like me?
- It's not possible, Michael.

623
00:36:44,775 --> 00:36:47,774
- How do you know?
- Some things you just know.

624
00:36:47,775 --> 00:36:50,854
On the other hand,
you might not like him.

625
00:36:50,855 --> 00:36:52,534
In which case, we'll run.

626
00:36:52,535 --> 00:36:53,895
(LAUGHING)

627
00:37:00,535 --> 00:37:02,525
Thank you. Thanks.

628
00:37:03,775 --> 00:37:05,334
Right, you're sure
you don't want tea, Michael?

629
00:37:05,335 --> 00:37:07,534
- That's okay.
- He does tend to drink water.

630
00:37:07,535 --> 00:37:09,895
It's fine. I want water.

631
00:37:09,975 --> 00:37:11,095
Okay.

632
00:37:12,255 --> 00:37:14,174
Oh, well, can you manage the top?

633
00:37:14,175 --> 00:37:16,721
- Yeah.
- Okay. Yeah. Thanks.

634
00:37:20,335 --> 00:37:21,405
Thank you.

635
00:37:23,095 --> 00:37:24,254
What's your best subject?

636
00:37:24,255 --> 00:37:25,614
I like maths best.

637
00:37:25,615 --> 00:37:28,854
Oh, well, that was my favourite subject.

638
00:37:28,855 --> 00:37:30,495
Was it your best?

639
00:37:31,655 --> 00:37:35,125
No. No, physics was my best subject.

640
00:37:35,535 --> 00:37:39,172
We don't do physics
until the big school.

641
00:37:39,375 --> 00:37:43,012
Well, maybe I didn't till my big school.

642
00:37:43,575 --> 00:37:47,414
- Why did you become a surgeon?
- I'm not sure.

643
00:37:47,415 --> 00:37:49,254
I hated dissection at school.

644
00:37:49,255 --> 00:37:51,334
Always thought
it was cruel and pointless.

645
00:37:51,335 --> 00:37:52,615
So did I.

646
00:37:53,055 --> 00:37:56,174
I ran out of class
when they started cutting up frogs.

647
00:37:56,175 --> 00:37:57,614
Did they use real animals?

648
00:37:57,615 --> 00:37:59,334
Oh, yeah, that's what they were.

649
00:37:59,335 --> 00:38:00,774
What did your teacher say?

650
00:38:00,775 --> 00:38:03,734
I made such a fuss,
they stopped using the animals.

651
00:38:03,735 --> 00:38:08,254
Good for you. I like people
who stand up for their beliefs.

652
00:38:08,255 --> 00:38:11,654
Mum and I went on a protest march
against hospital cuts.

653
00:38:11,655 --> 00:38:14,110
Was Mum active politically?

654
00:38:14,135 --> 00:38:17,094
- I don't know.
- But you went on demonstrations?

655
00:38:17,095 --> 00:38:18,641
Sometimes we did.

656
00:38:19,415 --> 00:38:22,234
Did you do everything together?

657
00:38:23,415 --> 00:38:25,052
I don't like this.

658
00:38:25,055 --> 00:38:27,974
- Would you like something else?
- No, it's all right.

659
00:38:27,975 --> 00:38:32,521
- You don't have to be so polite.
- I know I don't.

660
00:38:34,455 --> 00:38:36,894
You're very forthright, Michael.

661
00:38:36,895 --> 00:38:38,855
Is that good or bad?

662
00:38:39,495 --> 00:38:42,805
I'd... I'd say it was good, mostly.

663
00:38:45,255 --> 00:38:47,645
(PeOPLe CHATTeRING)

664
00:38:51,655 --> 00:38:54,575
(CHATTeRING)

665
00:38:54,655 --> 00:38:55,855
Under water?

666
00:38:57,615 --> 00:38:59,854
I'm just gonna...
I'll see you in a minute, okay?

667
00:38:59,855 --> 00:39:01,055
Yeah.

668
00:39:08,655 --> 00:39:09,975
(eXCLAIMS)

669
00:39:10,055 --> 00:39:12,054
- He is bright.
- Mmm-hmm.

670
00:39:12,055 --> 00:39:14,774
- He'll make a great barrister.
- Oh, is that what he wants to do?

671
00:39:14,775 --> 00:39:18,374
He talked about it
when his mum was alive.

672
00:39:18,375 --> 00:39:20,194
Did she suffer much?

673
00:39:20,735 --> 00:39:23,554
She didn't complain if she did.

674
00:39:26,335 --> 00:39:29,174
You're very coping, taking on Michael.

675
00:39:29,175 --> 00:39:32,734
It's not hard to see
why you've grown so fond of him.

676
00:39:32,735 --> 00:39:36,614
I wish Mimi was here.
It's more fun with her.

677
00:39:36,615 --> 00:39:38,334
Why? Who's Mimi? Your daughter?

678
00:39:38,335 --> 00:39:42,063
No. She's a dog.
She belongs to the Judge.

679
00:39:42,975 --> 00:39:44,734
Is he someone special?

680
00:39:44,735 --> 00:39:46,826
He was my pupil master.

681
00:39:47,255 --> 00:39:50,074
Is there something between you?

682
00:39:50,655 --> 00:39:55,655
You said Michael was quite forthright.
So are you, Marc.

683
00:40:17,295 --> 00:40:19,205
Night-night, Michael.

684
00:40:29,695 --> 00:40:32,241
He went right back to sleep.

685
00:40:38,895 --> 00:40:41,654
Thank you for making
our meeting so enjoyable.

686
00:40:41,655 --> 00:40:46,374
Well, I expected it to take place in the
council offices, with time running out,

687
00:40:46,375 --> 00:40:50,054
and Michael having got
no impression of me or me of him.

688
00:40:50,055 --> 00:40:52,601
What impression do you have?

689
00:40:57,295 --> 00:41:00,254
When I went to pay the bill
at the hotel,

690
00:41:00,255 --> 00:41:02,346
I watched you together.

691
00:41:02,815 --> 00:41:06,934
And you were so caught up in your world,
I thought I could never find a way in.

692
00:41:06,935 --> 00:41:09,117
I should have just left.

693
00:41:10,135 --> 00:41:12,317
It might be hard to now.

694
00:41:14,415 --> 00:41:17,054
Why didn't you make things work
for Diana Hulsey?

695
00:41:17,055 --> 00:41:19,328
She badly needed someone.

696
00:41:22,695 --> 00:41:23,975
I was...

697
00:41:25,455 --> 00:41:29,334
I was struggling with my own marriage
at the time.

698
00:41:29,335 --> 00:41:34,654
And just as you think
you've turned one corner in your life,

699
00:41:34,655 --> 00:41:38,201
you realise what corners you've missed.

700
00:41:39,895 --> 00:41:41,415
I'd better go.

701
00:41:42,135 --> 00:41:43,954
Are you coming back?

702
00:41:45,295 --> 00:41:47,015
Do you want me to?

703
00:41:50,215 --> 00:41:53,943
It'll be hurtful for Michael
if you don't.

704
00:41:54,215 --> 00:41:55,534
Can we have lunch tomorrow?

705
00:41:55,535 --> 00:41:58,734
- Be a sandwich at court.
- Can I watch you again?

706
00:41:58,735 --> 00:42:00,554
It's a public court.

707
00:42:04,735 --> 00:42:06,814
Mrs Mills,
the Judge is going to be late.

708
00:42:06,815 --> 00:42:09,774
- He's got something else on the list.
- Thank you, Mrs Cooper.

709
00:42:09,775 --> 00:42:13,134
He'd like you and Mr Norwalk
to join him for lunch at the lodgings.

710
00:42:13,135 --> 00:42:16,614
Oh, I'm sorry, I have a lunch date
with Michael's father.

711
00:42:16,615 --> 00:42:18,135
I'll tell him.

712
00:42:22,375 --> 00:42:23,735
All rise.

713
00:42:29,935 --> 00:42:32,295
Mr Stafford Clark,

714
00:42:32,375 --> 00:42:36,214
did the council's database
reveal anything interesting?

715
00:42:36,215 --> 00:42:39,397
Well, we didn't find a smoking gun.

716
00:42:39,535 --> 00:42:42,374
But we still argue that Mrs Hurst

717
00:42:42,375 --> 00:42:46,294
was not told the full extent
of her son's background.

718
00:42:46,295 --> 00:42:48,614
My lord, this is not
a sustainable position.

719
00:42:48,615 --> 00:42:51,214
The fact is the council revealed
all that was necessary.

720
00:42:51,215 --> 00:42:55,814
Mrs Channing, when a child as damaged
as Paul Lawrence is put up for adoption,

721
00:42:55,815 --> 00:42:59,134
the council should be
as open as it can be

722
00:42:59,135 --> 00:43:04,414
in reporting to prospective parents
the details of his personality.

723
00:43:04,415 --> 00:43:06,052
Mr Stafford Clark.

724
00:43:06,095 --> 00:43:12,054
It is our contention that the council
negligently withheld information.

725
00:43:12,055 --> 00:43:18,294
Telling a parent that her son is a liar,
a thief and a potential rapist

726
00:43:18,295 --> 00:43:20,654
is a very different matter
from telling a stranger.

727
00:43:20,655 --> 00:43:25,134
The stranger's judgement
would obviously be harsher.

728
00:43:25,135 --> 00:43:27,614
By the time Mrs Hurst had received

729
00:43:27,615 --> 00:43:30,734
the majority of the information
on Paul Lawrence,

730
00:43:30,735 --> 00:43:36,414
she had formed such a bond with him
as to be his de facto parent.

731
00:43:36,415 --> 00:43:42,143
This was brought about by deception
on the part of Mrs Tredaway.

732
00:43:43,815 --> 00:43:47,334
To call releasing information
piecemeal a deception

733
00:43:47,335 --> 00:43:50,063
is both emotive and unhelpful.

734
00:43:50,415 --> 00:43:54,894
The first duty the council has
is to protect the child up for adoption.

735
00:43:54,895 --> 00:43:57,414
When two adults wish to adopt,

736
00:43:57,415 --> 00:44:00,334
the council helps them decide
on a child.

737
00:44:00,335 --> 00:44:04,814
In this particular case, the parents
were steered away from Paul Lawrence.

738
00:44:04,815 --> 00:44:08,815
To quote Lady Justice Hale
in the essex case,

739
00:44:08,855 --> 00:44:11,614
"The agency for adoption
has a duty of care

740
00:44:11,615 --> 00:44:15,054
"in respect of what information
should be passed on.

741
00:44:15,055 --> 00:44:18,654
"It must at least have a policy
on divulging information.

742
00:44:18,655 --> 00:44:20,654
"But, clearly, it may be appropriate

743
00:44:20,655 --> 00:44:23,974
"to depart from that policy
in individual cases,

744
00:44:23,975 --> 00:44:27,734
"withholding information
which would otherwise be given.

745
00:44:27,735 --> 00:44:32,174
"That decision might well be made
on the advice of the adoption panel."

746
00:44:32,175 --> 00:44:35,414
Was the decision made after such advice,
Mrs Channing?

747
00:44:35,415 --> 00:44:37,597
- It was, my lord.
- Mmm.

748
00:44:39,575 --> 00:44:41,734
Either way, Mrs Hurst
seems to have suffered greatly

749
00:44:41,735 --> 00:44:45,099
from her involvement with this child.

750
00:44:45,655 --> 00:44:47,334
I will adjourn to consider my ruling.

751
00:44:47,335 --> 00:44:51,699
My clerk will inform you
when I've arrived at it.

752
00:44:52,255 --> 00:44:53,935
Usher: All rise.

753
00:44:57,735 --> 00:44:59,294
JO ON ANSWeRING MACHINe:
Hello, tHis is Jo Mills.

754
00:44:59,295 --> 00:45:00,974
Please leave a message
and I'll call you back.

755
00:45:00,975 --> 00:45:01,975
(BeePING)

756
00:45:02,055 --> 00:45:03,174
Jo, it's John.

757
00:45:03,175 --> 00:45:06,294
I need to take your mind
on the other case I'm hearing.

758
00:45:06,295 --> 00:45:09,934
Something you said about Mrs Hurst
and her suffering?

759
00:45:09,935 --> 00:45:11,454
Please, could you spare me
a couple of moments

760
00:45:11,455 --> 00:45:13,175
after court today?

761
00:45:17,015 --> 00:45:19,135
Come on.

762
00:45:21,655 --> 00:45:24,574
Did you get any sense
of Paul Lawrence's intention

763
00:45:24,575 --> 00:45:26,054
at the end of your class?

764
00:45:26,055 --> 00:45:28,525
No, he'd been responding

765
00:45:28,615 --> 00:45:30,734
and I thought
I was getting through to him.

766
00:45:30,735 --> 00:45:32,645
Did he stay behind?

767
00:45:32,735 --> 00:45:36,045
Yes. The lunch bell had sounded.

768
00:45:36,135 --> 00:45:40,414
Paul's class was on first sitting,
so they all rushed out.

769
00:45:40,415 --> 00:45:43,934
Paul stayed back and asked me
a question about the lesson.

770
00:45:43,935 --> 00:45:46,494
I was flattered that he'd listened.

771
00:45:46,495 --> 00:45:50,495
When I lent over the desk
to explain further,

772
00:45:50,815 --> 00:45:53,655
he squeezed my breast.

773
00:45:53,735 --> 00:45:55,554
How did you respond?

774
00:45:55,615 --> 00:45:58,214
I was shocked, but then I felt panic

775
00:45:58,215 --> 00:46:03,174
because I didn't want to lose him
now he was willing to learn.

776
00:46:03,175 --> 00:46:04,405
He said,

777
00:46:05,135 --> 00:46:09,226
"Oh, I thought you liked me.
No one likes me."

778
00:46:09,535 --> 00:46:12,990
And he caught his breath as if to sob.

779
00:46:13,255 --> 00:46:15,614
I put my hand on his shoulder
and I said,

780
00:46:15,615 --> 00:46:20,815
"I do like you, Paul,
but you mustn't touch me like that."

781
00:46:20,895 --> 00:46:23,986
And he said that he was very sorry

782
00:46:25,015 --> 00:46:27,135
and he took my hand.

783
00:46:28,695 --> 00:46:33,454
Then he suddenly grabbed me
and wrestled me to the floor.

784
00:46:33,455 --> 00:46:35,254
And I hit my head as I fell.

785
00:46:35,255 --> 00:46:36,854
(SIMPSON INHALeS DeePLY)

786
00:46:36,855 --> 00:46:40,695
Then he started pulling open my clothes.

787
00:46:40,775 --> 00:46:42,866
He's no bigger than me,

788
00:46:42,895 --> 00:46:48,375
but he kept me pinned down
with his hand over my mouth.

789
00:46:48,455 --> 00:46:51,655
- Could you make any sound?
- No.

790
00:46:51,735 --> 00:46:53,554
Not even afterwards.

791
00:46:54,215 --> 00:46:57,943
I just laid there
feeling sick with shock.

792
00:46:59,415 --> 00:47:01,574
I must have been there
for about 20 minutes,

793
00:47:01,575 --> 00:47:03,894
then a colleague came in and found me.

794
00:47:03,895 --> 00:47:06,934
We were supposed to meet at lunch.

795
00:47:06,935 --> 00:47:08,694
What did you imagine you were doing,

796
00:47:08,695 --> 00:47:11,054
putting your hand on a child
who you allege

797
00:47:11,055 --> 00:47:12,694
made a sexual advance to you?

798
00:47:12,695 --> 00:47:16,574
Oh, I don't know, I was...
I was trying to reassure him.

799
00:47:16,575 --> 00:47:20,774
Miss Simpson, you consented to this act
by Paul Lawrence, didn't you?

800
00:47:20,775 --> 00:47:22,335
No. No, I didn't.

801
00:47:22,415 --> 00:47:24,574
In fact, you enticed him into it.

802
00:47:24,575 --> 00:47:29,094
Then afterwards, you felt you somehow
had to cover what you had done,

803
00:47:29,095 --> 00:47:30,934
so you accused him of raping you.

804
00:47:30,935 --> 00:47:31,935
(CRYING)

805
00:47:32,015 --> 00:47:34,561
No, I didn't. It's not true.

806
00:47:37,855 --> 00:47:42,534
Dr Niamba, I believe you pronounced
Barry Baylis dead.

807
00:47:42,535 --> 00:47:43,855
How was that?

808
00:47:43,935 --> 00:47:46,214
I was holding my clinic
at the prison hospital

809
00:47:46,215 --> 00:47:48,574
when I heard the continuous alarm.

810
00:47:48,575 --> 00:47:50,574
Did you stop treatments?

811
00:47:50,575 --> 00:47:54,374
Not until a prison officer rushed in
and said a prisoner was down.

812
00:47:54,375 --> 00:47:56,285
That's code for dead.

813
00:47:56,455 --> 00:48:00,054
I ran after him
and found Baylis on the corridor floor

814
00:48:00,055 --> 00:48:02,054
with baler twine around his neck.

815
00:48:02,055 --> 00:48:04,334
I thought he'd hanged himself.

816
00:48:04,335 --> 00:48:07,335
I could tell at once he was dead.

817
00:48:08,455 --> 00:48:12,414
How long does it take to choke someone
in the way you describe?

818
00:48:12,415 --> 00:48:14,615
Not long. Two minutes.

819
00:48:14,695 --> 00:48:17,974
How long from hearing the alarm
to finding Baylis?

820
00:48:17,975 --> 00:48:20,335
Two minutes. No more.

821
00:48:20,415 --> 00:48:22,294
Didn't you examine him?

822
00:48:22,295 --> 00:48:25,614
- I certainly did.
- A brainstem death protocol?

823
00:48:25,615 --> 00:48:28,214
I checked his pulse and his breath.

824
00:48:28,215 --> 00:48:29,695
He was dead.

825
00:48:30,855 --> 00:48:34,454
And you took longer from the alarm
to finding Baylis.

826
00:48:34,455 --> 00:48:36,254
Maybe five or six minutes.

827
00:48:36,255 --> 00:48:38,346
No, we ran all the way.

828
00:48:38,375 --> 00:48:40,014
Did you try to revive him?

829
00:48:40,015 --> 00:48:41,455
He was dead.

830
00:48:43,015 --> 00:48:45,485
Dr Niamba, at two minutes,

831
00:48:45,575 --> 00:48:48,414
a person who is choking
loses consciousness.

832
00:48:48,415 --> 00:48:52,375
It takes five or six minutes
to kill them.

833
00:48:52,455 --> 00:48:54,495
Isn't that so?

834
00:48:54,575 --> 00:48:55,895
Maybe.

835
00:49:00,695 --> 00:49:02,055
Perhaps.

836
00:49:02,135 --> 00:49:03,895
(PeOPLe MURMURING)

837
00:49:05,975 --> 00:49:09,814
Were you aware of hostility
amongst prisoners towards Baylis?

838
00:49:09,815 --> 00:49:11,375
Yes.

839
00:49:11,455 --> 00:49:13,135
A few hated him.

840
00:49:14,375 --> 00:49:18,254
There were three guards on that block,
two with first aid,

841
00:49:18,255 --> 00:49:21,528
yet none attempted to revive Baylis?

842
00:49:22,255 --> 00:49:23,695
No.

843
00:49:24,575 --> 00:49:26,095
No one did.

844
00:49:27,015 --> 00:49:30,174
Pretty short lunch.
I thought judges led extravagant lives.

845
00:49:30,175 --> 00:49:33,054
Not in this century,
and certainly not with John Deed.

846
00:49:33,055 --> 00:49:36,214
Oh, yeah, this is the judge
with the dog that Michael likes.

847
00:49:36,215 --> 00:49:38,574
- Mmm-hmm.
- Hmm, perhaps we should steal it.

848
00:49:38,575 --> 00:49:39,814
Can the Judge run fast?

849
00:49:39,815 --> 00:49:43,374
(LAUGHS) For a cardiac surgeon,
you have a lot of mischief in you.

850
00:49:43,375 --> 00:49:45,694
I have to work at it.
You know people take themselves

851
00:49:45,695 --> 00:49:47,415
far too seriously.

852
00:49:48,055 --> 00:49:50,874
Why did your marriage break up?

853
00:49:51,615 --> 00:49:54,161
Uh, I'm sorry, it's painful.

854
00:49:54,895 --> 00:49:57,895
I was, um... I was work-obsessed.

855
00:49:58,815 --> 00:50:01,414
By the time I realised there was
more to life than driving ambition,

856
00:50:01,415 --> 00:50:03,135
Louisa had gone.

857
00:50:03,215 --> 00:50:04,854
Couldn't you get her back?

858
00:50:04,855 --> 00:50:08,128
- No, she found somebody else.
- Hmm.

859
00:50:08,735 --> 00:50:11,008
She didn't want the boys.

860
00:50:11,015 --> 00:50:13,405
They took that very badly.

861
00:50:13,495 --> 00:50:15,375
Oh, we all did.

862
00:50:15,455 --> 00:50:18,546
But, um, we get over these things.

863
00:50:18,735 --> 00:50:21,554
They're very, very settled now.

864
00:50:23,535 --> 00:50:25,172
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

865
00:50:25,215 --> 00:50:27,761
- Mrs Mills, Judge.
- Thanks.

866
00:50:27,975 --> 00:50:30,134
Thanks, Coop, could we have tea?

867
00:50:30,135 --> 00:50:32,374
I can't stop,
I've got to get back for Michael.

868
00:50:32,375 --> 00:50:34,334
I seem to be seeing less of you now.

869
00:50:34,335 --> 00:50:36,454
You shouldn't see me at all
while I'm in front of you.

870
00:50:36,455 --> 00:50:40,454
Perhaps I should give up the law.
I want to see more of both of you.

871
00:50:40,455 --> 00:50:42,574
You asked about Mrs Hurst.

872
00:50:42,575 --> 00:50:46,934
Yes, you said you had some insight
into her taking on this difficult trial,

873
00:50:46,935 --> 00:50:49,734
you know, subconsciously wanting
to destroy herself and her family.

874
00:50:49,735 --> 00:50:51,334
It was only a surmise, John.

875
00:50:51,335 --> 00:50:53,174
Only it seems
that she's about to self-destruct

876
00:50:53,175 --> 00:50:54,694
and I don't want to
exacerbate her problem,

877
00:50:54,695 --> 00:50:57,614
but I don't see
that I have much alternative.

878
00:50:57,615 --> 00:50:58,765
Mmm.

879
00:51:03,975 --> 00:51:05,495
Sorry.

880
00:51:05,575 --> 00:51:08,454
I could come
and collect Michael with you.

881
00:51:08,455 --> 00:51:10,854
- Mimi misses him.
- No, it would be confusing for him.

882
00:51:10,855 --> 00:51:12,494
His father will be there.

883
00:51:12,495 --> 00:51:14,654
Ah, yes, what's he like?

884
00:51:14,655 --> 00:51:17,575
Um, warm, forthright.

885
00:51:18,415 --> 00:51:22,214
(LAUGHS) It's like
love at first sight between them.

886
00:51:22,215 --> 00:51:25,397
It's like they've never been apart.

887
00:51:26,455 --> 00:51:29,494
He's like you. He's bright, hardworking.

888
00:51:29,495 --> 00:51:31,254
Put himself through college
and medical school.

889
00:51:31,255 --> 00:51:32,734
He's a cardiac surgeon.

890
00:51:32,735 --> 00:51:35,535
Has he stolen your heart, Jo?

891
00:51:35,615 --> 00:51:37,375
I must dash.

892
00:51:42,295 --> 00:51:43,895
(DOOR CLOSING)

893
00:51:48,935 --> 00:51:51,054
It was a wonderful evening.

894
00:51:51,055 --> 00:51:53,734
- We didn't do anything.
- But that's what was so nice.

895
00:51:53,735 --> 00:51:55,695
We didn't have to.

896
00:51:55,775 --> 00:51:58,594
Even the silences felt natural.

897
00:52:00,855 --> 00:52:04,492
So what are we going to do
about Michael?

898
00:52:05,575 --> 00:52:07,214
It's not a done deal.

899
00:52:07,215 --> 00:52:11,254
The council have only indicated
that you could get custody.

900
00:52:11,255 --> 00:52:13,325
I could fight you.

901
00:52:14,095 --> 00:52:17,005
What would Michael make of that?

902
00:52:18,055 --> 00:52:21,146
I don't want to give him up, Marc.

903
00:52:21,975 --> 00:52:24,521
Do you want me to walk away?

904
00:52:29,935 --> 00:52:31,454
JO ON ANSWeRING MACHINe:
Hello, tHis is Jo Mills.

905
00:52:31,455 --> 00:52:34,414
Please leave a message
and I'll call you back...

906
00:52:34,415 --> 00:52:35,735
(SIGHING)

907
00:52:42,895 --> 00:52:44,255
Come on.

908
00:53:18,295 --> 00:53:21,568
Mr Ferns, did you kill Barry Baylis?

909
00:53:25,695 --> 00:53:27,255
Yes, I did.

910
00:53:27,735 --> 00:53:30,614
I strangled him
with a piece of baler twine

911
00:53:30,615 --> 00:53:32,534
I was using as a belt.

912
00:53:32,535 --> 00:53:34,354
Why did you do that?

913
00:53:34,375 --> 00:53:38,285
To stop him ever abusing
any other children.

914
00:53:40,335 --> 00:53:44,334
Seven years ago, I went to prison
for attacking a paedophile.

915
00:53:44,335 --> 00:53:48,535
In that time,
the organisation I helped found,

916
00:53:48,615 --> 00:53:51,343
Lawyers Against Child Abusers,

917
00:53:51,415 --> 00:53:53,654
estimate there have been over 90,000

918
00:53:53,655 --> 00:53:56,294
serious offences
committed against children.

919
00:53:56,295 --> 00:54:01,094
Less than one percent of those offenders
have been prosecuted.

920
00:54:01,095 --> 00:54:05,368
Can you tell the court
about your own childhood?

921
00:54:09,655 --> 00:54:11,295
Mr Ferns?

922
00:54:16,735 --> 00:54:21,094
My father had a breakdown when I was two
and walked out on us.

923
00:54:21,095 --> 00:54:23,368
I haven't seen him since.

924
00:54:24,415 --> 00:54:27,415
The man my mother took up with...

925
00:54:28,935 --> 00:54:31,254
He started sexually abusing me
when I was five.

926
00:54:31,255 --> 00:54:34,885
I was nine before I dared tell anyone.

927
00:54:34,975 --> 00:54:36,814
JO: What happened to him?

928
00:54:36,815 --> 00:54:38,045
Nothing.

929
00:54:39,015 --> 00:54:42,294
There wasn't enough evidence
to prosecute him.

930
00:54:42,295 --> 00:54:45,374
By this time my mother
had become a drug addict

931
00:54:45,375 --> 00:54:47,739
and I was taken into care.

932
00:54:48,175 --> 00:54:51,374
One of the carers started to abuse me.

933
00:54:51,375 --> 00:54:54,648
I thought all adults were like that.

934
00:54:55,415 --> 00:55:00,870
I tried to commit suicide
and ended up in a psychiatric unit.

935
00:55:01,335 --> 00:55:02,774
There I met some kind adults

936
00:55:02,775 --> 00:55:05,094
who helped me get
a different view of the world.

937
00:55:05,095 --> 00:55:07,574
JO: And what of the men who abused you?

938
00:55:07,575 --> 00:55:10,694
I tracked them down
and exposed each one of them.

939
00:55:10,695 --> 00:55:12,494
Were they prosecuted?

940
00:55:12,495 --> 00:55:13,565
No.

941
00:55:14,495 --> 00:55:17,574
The law couldn't do anything
because of lack of evidence.

942
00:55:17,575 --> 00:55:19,535
So, I beat them up.

943
00:55:20,935 --> 00:55:24,214
One almost died
and I went to prison for seven years.

944
00:55:24,215 --> 00:55:27,374
How much of your prison sentence
did you serve?

945
00:55:27,375 --> 00:55:29,365
All but a day.

946
00:55:29,455 --> 00:55:31,814
They wouldn't give me remission
because I refused to admit

947
00:55:31,815 --> 00:55:35,543
that what I did to those people
was wrong.

948
00:55:35,775 --> 00:55:40,334
Your childhood experiences
must have left you very angry.

949
00:55:40,335 --> 00:55:43,154
I won't pretend I wasn't angry.

950
00:55:43,215 --> 00:55:47,374
And particularly resentful
towards your abusers

951
00:55:47,375 --> 00:55:49,557
who didn't go to prison.

952
00:55:50,175 --> 00:55:51,895
Is that so?

953
00:55:51,975 --> 00:55:53,255
At first.

954
00:55:54,135 --> 00:55:56,294
And then I realised
I couldn't take it upon myself

955
00:55:56,295 --> 00:55:58,974
to beat up every child abuser,

956
00:55:58,975 --> 00:56:00,974
so that's why I read law,

957
00:56:00,975 --> 00:56:04,134
got a degree, founded Lawyers Against,

958
00:56:04,135 --> 00:56:07,614
to prosecute these people
when the authorities won't.

959
00:56:07,615 --> 00:56:12,054
Isn't this organisation
simply a front to out paedophiles

960
00:56:12,055 --> 00:56:14,614
who you can later vent your anger on?

961
00:56:14,615 --> 00:56:18,454
Ask some of the lawyers
who've joined us if it is a front.

962
00:56:18,455 --> 00:56:21,574
What were you thinking
when you attacked Baylis?

963
00:56:21,575 --> 00:56:23,854
How he'd never get to harm
another child.

964
00:56:23,855 --> 00:56:26,334
What evidence had you got
that made you superior

965
00:56:26,335 --> 00:56:28,814
to the courts and due process?

966
00:56:28,815 --> 00:56:31,974
Oh, we'd made enquiries,
we knew he was a child abuser.

967
00:56:31,975 --> 00:56:35,414
- But you had no evidence.
- Well, it takes time.

968
00:56:35,415 --> 00:56:37,615
And he was very clever.

969
00:56:37,695 --> 00:56:40,134
That's why he kept escaping the law.

970
00:56:40,135 --> 00:56:44,045
You haven't forgiven your abusers,
have you?

971
00:56:44,295 --> 00:56:45,374
What do you think?

972
00:56:45,375 --> 00:56:49,414
I think you'd like to do to them
what you did to Mr Baylis.

973
00:56:49,415 --> 00:56:52,143
I try not to think about them.

974
00:56:52,175 --> 00:56:56,095
I think you think about them
all the time.

975
00:56:56,175 --> 00:56:57,494
When you attacked Mr Baylis,

976
00:56:57,495 --> 00:57:01,814
you were thinking about
your stepfather and carer.

977
00:57:01,815 --> 00:57:04,654
No, I just wanted to stop Baylis.

978
00:57:04,655 --> 00:57:07,694
You weren't protecting other kids
when you attacked and killed this man.

979
00:57:07,695 --> 00:57:10,734
You were just hell-bent on revenge!
Weren't you?

980
00:57:10,735 --> 00:57:12,917
No. No, that's not true.

981
00:57:14,495 --> 00:57:18,174
We'll let the jury decide what's true,
shall we?

982
00:57:18,175 --> 00:57:20,374
Do you know what happens
when you suffer that kind of abuse?

983
00:57:20,375 --> 00:57:23,414
- Thank you, Mr Ferns.
- You die inside.

984
00:57:23,415 --> 00:57:25,614
That's what happened to me.

985
00:57:25,615 --> 00:57:29,214
Abused children never really recover
to become fully functioning.

986
00:57:29,215 --> 00:57:31,414
- Thank you...
- It's like you're not worthy inside.

987
00:57:31,415 --> 00:57:36,214
You're dirty. You're not worthy
of anyone's love and respect.

988
00:57:36,215 --> 00:57:41,494
My life now is about not letting
that happen to other children.

989
00:57:41,495 --> 00:57:42,694
I don't... Thank you, Mr Johnson.

990
00:57:42,695 --> 00:57:44,694
I don't want to hear
"in defence of others"

991
00:57:44,695 --> 00:57:45,974
in your closing argument, Jo.

992
00:57:45,975 --> 00:57:48,574
- Those are my client's instructions.
- The law is clear.

993
00:57:48,575 --> 00:57:49,654
No one's being threatened.

994
00:57:49,655 --> 00:57:51,254
The jury should be allowed to decide.

995
00:57:51,255 --> 00:57:53,934
Yes, and they will.
With direction from me.

996
00:57:53,935 --> 00:57:56,894
Juries can get very exercised
about paedophiles.

997
00:57:56,895 --> 00:57:59,532
The jury may yet surprise us.

998
00:58:04,615 --> 00:58:06,254
Don't you ever answer your phone?

999
00:58:06,255 --> 00:58:08,214
It's been a hectic few days.

1000
00:58:08,215 --> 00:58:10,934
Michael's father must be keen.

1001
00:58:10,935 --> 00:58:13,454
The council told him
he could claim Michael.

1002
00:58:13,455 --> 00:58:15,735
They'll support him.

1003
00:58:15,815 --> 00:58:19,014
Is he going to take Michael
back to South Africa?

1004
00:58:19,015 --> 00:58:21,934
He has to go back soon,
he has other children there.

1005
00:58:21,935 --> 00:58:24,390
He can't just take Michael.

1006
00:58:24,975 --> 00:58:27,430
He wants me to go with him.

1007
00:58:40,735 --> 00:58:43,463
It's always warm in Cape Town.

1008
00:58:44,615 --> 00:58:47,494
I'm really glad
you found your father, Michael.

1009
00:58:47,495 --> 00:58:48,934
Won't you come with us?

1010
00:58:48,935 --> 00:58:52,294
I didn't say I wouldn't. It's just
it needs a lot of thinking about.

1011
00:58:52,295 --> 00:58:55,214
I've got Tom and Mark
to think about as well as you.

1012
00:58:55,215 --> 00:58:56,615
And my work.

1013
00:58:56,695 --> 00:58:59,374
Dad says you can be a barrister
in South Africa.

1014
00:58:59,375 --> 00:59:00,894
(LAUGHING) Oh, does he?

1015
00:59:00,895 --> 00:59:03,350
He's got it all worked out.

1016
00:59:04,015 --> 00:59:06,015
Will you come with us?

1017
00:59:08,375 --> 00:59:09,695
(SIGHING)

1018
00:59:11,775 --> 00:59:14,245
Michael, if I don't,

1019
00:59:16,255 --> 00:59:19,334
it doesn't mean I don't love you.
All right?

1020
00:59:19,335 --> 00:59:22,245
It's just a really big upheaval.

1021
00:59:23,375 --> 00:59:25,734
Dad says we could all make a new start.

1022
00:59:25,735 --> 00:59:27,415
It would be best.

1023
00:59:28,295 --> 00:59:30,975
Yeah, perhaps it would.

1024
00:59:34,895 --> 00:59:36,215
(SIGHING)

1025
00:59:38,735 --> 00:59:40,294
JO ON ANSWeRING MACHINe:
Hello, tHis is Jo Mills.

1026
00:59:40,295 --> 00:59:44,114
Please leave a message
and I'll call you...

1027
00:59:50,575 --> 00:59:51,734
excuse me, Sir John.

1028
00:59:51,735 --> 00:59:54,894
Cook wants to know if you're dining in.

1029
00:59:54,895 --> 00:59:56,854
Is Mr Justice everard in?

1030
00:59:56,855 --> 00:59:59,414
Sir Monty and Lady everard
are at a reception.

1031
00:59:59,415 --> 01:00:03,961
Um, no thank you, Mr Johnson,
I think I'll eat out.

1032
01:00:06,815 --> 01:00:09,335
(PeOPLe CHATTeRING)

1033
01:00:25,575 --> 01:00:27,121
(CLeARING THROAT)

1034
01:00:28,135 --> 01:00:30,414
Oh, Mrs Hurst,
I'm sorry, I didn't recognise you.

1035
01:00:30,415 --> 01:00:32,294
Can I talk to you, Judge?

1036
01:00:32,295 --> 01:00:33,814
I'm not sure that you can, Mrs Hurst.

1037
01:00:33,815 --> 01:00:36,134
The council weren't being honest.

1038
01:00:36,135 --> 01:00:39,934
There are rules about talking
to claimants outside court.

1039
01:00:39,935 --> 01:00:41,815
I'm sorry.

1040
01:00:41,895 --> 01:00:45,174
Just when I saw you,
I knew I needed to speak to you.

1041
01:00:45,175 --> 01:00:46,535
Please?

1042
01:00:47,295 --> 01:00:51,568
If I don't speak to someone,
I feel I'll go mad.

1043
01:00:52,975 --> 01:00:54,574
Would you like a glass of wine?

1044
01:00:54,575 --> 01:00:57,214
Uh, I don't want to take up your time.

1045
01:00:57,215 --> 01:00:59,488
It's boring eating alone.

1046
01:01:00,935 --> 01:01:03,845
- Are you eating alone?
- Always.

1047
01:01:04,175 --> 01:01:06,774
The girls live with their father.

1048
01:01:06,775 --> 01:01:09,594
Could you get me another glass?

1049
01:01:13,815 --> 01:01:15,255
Thank you.

1050
01:01:31,095 --> 01:01:34,494
Do the girls blame you
for what Paul did?

1051
01:01:34,495 --> 01:01:37,774
The eldest one barely talks to me now.

1052
01:01:37,775 --> 01:01:41,454
I didn't know he would abuse her.
Of course I didn't.

1053
01:01:41,455 --> 01:01:44,494
It doesn't necessarily
make the council negligent.

1054
01:01:44,495 --> 01:01:47,950
I thought we couldn't talk about this.

1055
01:01:50,495 --> 01:01:53,694
The girls can't understand
why I stood by him.

1056
01:01:53,695 --> 01:01:56,150
Neither could their father.

1057
01:01:57,575 --> 01:01:59,939
I've already lost one son.

1058
01:02:00,895 --> 01:02:04,294
I was driving the car when...
when he died.

1059
01:02:04,295 --> 01:02:06,214
He was telling me about his teacher.

1060
01:02:06,215 --> 01:02:07,695
Doing his voice.

1061
01:02:09,175 --> 01:02:11,448
He was a brilliant mimic.

1062
01:02:15,095 --> 01:02:19,368
I only took my eyes off the road
for a second...

1063
01:02:20,735 --> 01:02:23,214
It was an accident.
You should forgive yourself

1064
01:02:23,215 --> 01:02:25,454
- for what happened.
- No.

1065
01:02:25,455 --> 01:02:28,335
It was my fault. I killed him.

1066
01:02:29,535 --> 01:02:34,717
Can you accept the responsibility
without attaching blame?

1067
01:02:34,895 --> 01:02:37,614
Is that what you think I'm trying to do?

1068
01:02:37,615 --> 01:02:42,888
Perhaps you should care for the family
that you still have.

1069
01:02:44,775 --> 01:02:46,815
I can't abandon Paul.

1070
01:02:46,895 --> 01:02:50,441
Too many people have already done that.

1071
01:02:51,615 --> 01:02:55,894
It's a tough hurdle to overcome
when you're a child.

1072
01:02:55,895 --> 01:02:58,045
That happened to me.

1073
01:02:59,095 --> 01:03:02,654
I was adopted.
My adoptive mother committed suicide

1074
01:03:02,655 --> 01:03:05,837
when I was a bit younger than Paul.

1075
01:03:06,695 --> 01:03:12,968
I mean, it wasn't anybody's fault,
there wasn't anybody I could blame.

1076
01:03:18,015 --> 01:03:21,288
I think you're suffering, Mrs Hurst.

1077
01:03:23,455 --> 01:03:28,374
We would think that our day in court
is going to resolve things,

1078
01:03:28,375 --> 01:03:29,735
make us free.

1079
01:03:29,815 --> 01:03:32,734
We think it will bring closure.
Quite often,

1080
01:03:32,735 --> 01:03:35,735
it just opens wounds even deeper.

1081
01:03:37,855 --> 01:03:40,935
You need to go on with your life.

1082
01:03:41,015 --> 01:03:43,174
I don't know how to, Judge.

1083
01:03:43,175 --> 01:03:45,374
You've got to find a way.

1084
01:03:45,375 --> 01:03:48,934
At best, all a trial will do
is enable you to shift the blame

1085
01:03:48,935 --> 01:03:51,374
from yourself to the council.

1086
01:03:51,375 --> 01:03:53,134
The situation will remain the same.

1087
01:03:53,135 --> 01:03:55,534
Then what's the point of courts?

1088
01:03:55,535 --> 01:03:56,895
(LAUGHING)

1089
01:03:58,015 --> 01:04:04,125
Often they have no point
other than to codify our behaviour

1090
01:04:04,215 --> 01:04:07,974
because we don't know
how to relate to each other.

1091
01:04:07,975 --> 01:04:10,248
Do you think we ever did?

1092
01:04:15,255 --> 01:04:18,254
When was the last time
you had a conversation like this?

1093
01:04:18,255 --> 01:04:19,775
Oh...

1094
01:04:23,175 --> 01:04:24,695
I should go.

1095
01:04:24,775 --> 01:04:28,655
I think you should have
your glass of wine.

1096
01:04:28,735 --> 01:04:32,334
For the first time,
you look like you're not going to break.

1097
01:04:32,335 --> 01:04:37,335
Well, it is a relief
to talk to someone who understands.

1098
01:04:39,095 --> 01:04:41,814
- So, I'm defending a dog...
- Mmm-hmm.

1099
01:04:41,815 --> 01:04:45,534
...on appeal,
under the Dangerous Dogs Act

1100
01:04:45,535 --> 01:04:46,694
and it bit me.

1101
01:04:46,695 --> 01:04:48,295
(LAUGHING)

1102
01:04:48,375 --> 01:04:51,534
So I'm standing in front of this judge,
blood pouring from my hand,

1103
01:04:51,535 --> 01:04:54,974
and I'm trying to convince him
that this dog is not dangerous.

1104
01:04:54,975 --> 01:04:58,335
(LAUGHING)

1105
01:05:01,055 --> 01:05:04,654
You're a very different person
when you laugh.

1106
01:05:04,655 --> 01:05:10,814
- Oh, I haven't laughed for a long time.
- You should do it more often.

1107
01:05:10,815 --> 01:05:16,054
I haven't dared let myself
have an evening like this since...

1108
01:05:16,055 --> 01:05:19,328
I'd forgotten what it could be like.

1109
01:05:19,735 --> 01:05:21,495
I must go.

1110
01:05:27,255 --> 01:05:29,455
Where are you going?

1111
01:05:29,535 --> 01:05:32,626
Back to your flat to be depressed?

1112
01:05:34,735 --> 01:05:37,281
You want to make love to me.

1113
01:05:42,055 --> 01:05:46,874
It's very dangerous for me
even to have such thoughts.

1114
01:06:18,815 --> 01:06:22,854
Mr Johnson,
who is that woman going away in a taxi?

1115
01:06:22,855 --> 01:06:25,614
I believe she was a guest
of Mr Justice Deed, my lady.

1116
01:06:25,615 --> 01:06:27,975
No, I do hope she wasn't.

1117
01:06:28,575 --> 01:06:30,614
Did she come for breakfast?

1118
01:06:30,615 --> 01:06:34,134
I really don't know, my lady.
I don't serve breakfast.

1119
01:06:34,135 --> 01:06:39,894
Mr Johnson, you're the butler,
you're supposed to know everything.

1120
01:06:39,895 --> 01:06:41,655
Where's my husband?

1121
01:06:45,215 --> 01:06:48,845
Although Mrs Hurst, um, and her husband

1122
01:06:49,735 --> 01:06:55,294
were treated in less than ideal fashion
over the adoption of Paul Lawrence,

1123
01:06:55,295 --> 01:07:00,114
the council, I find,
was not wrong in its proceedings.

1124
01:07:00,815 --> 01:07:05,094
Enough information was given for them
to make a fair and reasoned judgement.

1125
01:07:05,095 --> 01:07:09,974
The fact that they then took Paul on
is very much to their credit.

1126
01:07:09,975 --> 01:07:16,655
That the adoption went so badly wrong
in no way reflects upon Mrs Hurst.

1127
01:07:17,695 --> 01:07:19,774
She has this court's heartfelt sympathy

1128
01:07:19,775 --> 01:07:24,134
and it is hoped that she can somehow
pick up her life.

1129
01:07:24,135 --> 01:07:27,255
The full text of this ruling

1130
01:07:27,335 --> 01:07:30,454
will be on the court service website
next week.

1131
01:07:30,455 --> 01:07:32,135
Usher: All rise.

1132
01:07:34,415 --> 01:07:36,095
John.

1133
01:07:36,175 --> 01:07:38,375
John, you all right?

1134
01:07:38,895 --> 01:07:43,805
- Was Mrs Hurst in your court today?
- No, she was not.

1135
01:07:43,815 --> 01:07:47,774
Vera says that she saw her
leaving the digs this morning.

1136
01:07:47,775 --> 01:07:49,334
Yes, she wanted some advice.

1137
01:07:49,335 --> 01:07:52,494
You are not the person to give it.
She's before you as a claimant.

1138
01:07:52,495 --> 01:07:54,894
Hopefully I was able to help her.

1139
01:07:54,895 --> 01:07:58,614
Well, the fact is
that she shouldn't have visited you.

1140
01:07:58,615 --> 01:08:00,614
As Presider, it is incumbent on me

1141
01:08:00,615 --> 01:08:02,814
to ensure that such liaisons
don't happen.

1142
01:08:02,815 --> 01:08:05,974
Yes, I know who I can
and cannot see, Monty.

1143
01:08:05,975 --> 01:08:07,794
It's very dangerous.

1144
01:08:08,215 --> 01:08:10,094
It could embarrass you and the bench.

1145
01:08:10,095 --> 01:08:14,695
Monty, I'm not sure that I give a damn!

1146
01:08:18,135 --> 01:08:21,414
Mr Justice Deed had sexual relations
with this woman?

1147
01:08:21,415 --> 01:08:24,174
I doubt they spent the time
discussing the price of coffee.

1148
01:08:24,175 --> 01:08:25,266
In his room?

1149
01:08:25,335 --> 01:08:28,334
I'm told the servants weren't asked
to make up a spare room.

1150
01:08:28,335 --> 01:08:30,534
This time we have him. We really do.

1151
01:08:30,535 --> 01:08:32,014
We've never been able to pin him down

1152
01:08:32,015 --> 01:08:34,254
on his sexual relations
with the female bar.

1153
01:08:34,255 --> 01:08:36,774
With a claimant, it's judicial suicide.

1154
01:08:36,775 --> 01:08:40,094
That's a dangerous assumption, Ian.
You have no evidence.

1155
01:08:40,095 --> 01:08:41,534
Vera: Come on, Monty.

1156
01:08:41,535 --> 01:08:44,934
The only way to find out if it's true
is to confront him, Monty.

1157
01:08:44,935 --> 01:08:47,815
- Good idea. Let's.
- No, Vera.

1158
01:08:48,255 --> 01:08:50,254
You'll do no such thing.

1159
01:08:50,255 --> 01:08:53,165
John Deed is a High Court Judge.

1160
01:08:53,655 --> 01:08:56,054
Ian can decide how to proceed.

1161
01:08:56,055 --> 01:08:57,965
I would urge caution.

1162
01:09:02,175 --> 01:09:06,094
It's a simple question.
Was this woman in your room?

1163
01:09:06,095 --> 01:09:08,732
It's not simple. It's loaded.

1164
01:09:08,975 --> 01:09:12,414
If true, it could do you
a great deal of harm.

1165
01:09:12,415 --> 01:09:14,775
Yes, I'm sure it could.

1166
01:09:14,855 --> 01:09:18,934
My lord, if the newspapers
were to get hold of this...

1167
01:09:18,935 --> 01:09:21,454
Only if you were to
give it to them, Ian.

1168
01:09:21,455 --> 01:09:24,974
It's your reputation
we're at pains to protect.

1169
01:09:24,975 --> 01:09:27,495
Thank you for your concern.

1170
01:09:32,535 --> 01:09:34,415
Mrs Cooper.

1171
01:09:40,695 --> 01:09:42,534
Did you hear any of that?

1172
01:09:42,535 --> 01:09:44,654
More than I wanted to, Judge.

1173
01:09:44,655 --> 01:09:45,894
There could be trouble.

1174
01:09:45,895 --> 01:09:49,125
(LAUGHS) We've had plenty of that.

1175
01:09:55,215 --> 01:09:57,494
Mrs Mills still in the building?

1176
01:09:57,495 --> 01:10:01,054
I saw her hurrying through the lobby
with that man.

1177
01:10:01,055 --> 01:10:05,419
She might be going to South Africa
with that man.

1178
01:10:05,695 --> 01:10:07,375
Really, Judge?

1179
01:10:08,895 --> 01:10:12,334
Find Dr Crawcheck for me, will you?
Ask her if she'll see me tonight.

1180
01:10:12,335 --> 01:10:14,517
It doesn't matter where.

1181
01:10:16,455 --> 01:10:17,495
Mimi.

1182
01:10:17,575 --> 01:10:18,725
(WHISTLeS)

1183
01:10:19,135 --> 01:10:21,415
(eNGINe ROARING)

1184
01:10:27,855 --> 01:10:29,005
Shit.

1185
01:10:36,255 --> 01:10:37,710
Is Michael here?

1186
01:10:38,375 --> 01:10:43,739
- I brought Mimi to see him.
- No, he's out with his father.

1187
01:10:44,935 --> 01:10:46,895
Hello, little one.

1188
01:10:48,495 --> 01:10:50,586
What's going to happen?

1189
01:10:55,055 --> 01:10:58,374
Michael wants me to go to South Africa.

1190
01:10:58,375 --> 01:11:00,012
I haven't decided.

1191
01:11:09,415 --> 01:11:10,565
John?

1192
01:11:12,135 --> 01:11:13,495
Talk to me.

1193
01:11:14,255 --> 01:11:16,614
I've been trying to talk to you
all week.

1194
01:11:16,615 --> 01:11:18,343
Can't get near you.

1195
01:11:18,775 --> 01:11:21,814
Marc's only here for a short time.

1196
01:11:21,815 --> 01:11:23,934
Are you in love with him?

1197
01:11:23,935 --> 01:11:27,295
I've only known him a few days.

1198
01:11:28,295 --> 01:11:29,695
He's very nice.

1199
01:11:30,095 --> 01:11:31,095
Charming.

1200
01:11:32,655 --> 01:11:34,094
He pays me a lot of attention.

1201
01:11:34,095 --> 01:11:38,415
(STAMMeRING) He sort of
assaults your feelings.

1202
01:12:10,095 --> 01:12:15,732
I've done something pretty stupid,
even by my stupid standards.

1203
01:12:19,655 --> 01:12:23,474
I let Mrs Hurst
stay the night at the digs.

1204
01:12:25,615 --> 01:12:26,815
(SIGHS)

1205
01:12:26,895 --> 01:12:29,535
Are you completely mad?

1206
01:12:29,895 --> 01:12:32,294
And the forces of reaction know.

1207
01:12:32,295 --> 01:12:35,174
Well, what do you expect me to do?
I can't help you.

1208
01:12:35,175 --> 01:12:37,294
Are you surprised I'm thinking
of going to South Africa?

1209
01:12:37,295 --> 01:12:39,015
You are so stupid!

1210
01:12:41,935 --> 01:12:44,655
Come on. Come on. Come on.

1211
01:12:48,495 --> 01:12:49,768
(DOOR CLOSING)

1212
01:12:50,815 --> 01:12:52,694
even by your exotic standards,

1213
01:12:52,695 --> 01:12:54,294
sleeping with a claimant
who is before you

1214
01:12:54,295 --> 01:12:56,934
seems a very stupid thing to do.

1215
01:12:56,935 --> 01:12:59,654
Well, I compounded the stupidity.

1216
01:12:59,655 --> 01:13:02,294
Instead of recusing myself,
I gave judgement.

1217
01:13:02,295 --> 01:13:04,055
Not in her favour.

1218
01:13:04,135 --> 01:13:05,494
That hardly matters.

1219
01:13:05,495 --> 01:13:08,495
It might if she's angry about it.

1220
01:13:11,655 --> 01:13:16,565
I think Jo Mills is falling in love
with somebody else.

1221
01:13:17,095 --> 01:13:19,414
So you've decided to destroy yourself.

1222
01:13:19,415 --> 01:13:21,095
Help me, Rachel.

1223
01:13:21,175 --> 01:13:24,334
How do you imagine I can do that
after sleeping with you?

1224
01:13:24,335 --> 01:13:28,574
How could we possibly re-establish
a client-therapist relationship?

1225
01:13:28,575 --> 01:13:29,694
We could find a way.

1226
01:13:29,695 --> 01:13:32,374
- Until we fall into sex again.
- Is that going to happen?

1227
01:13:32,375 --> 01:13:34,694
You're that sort of man, John.

1228
01:13:34,695 --> 01:13:38,605
Sex is some deep pathology
you're suffering.

1229
01:13:42,615 --> 01:13:46,375
Come on. Let's get some coffee and talk.

1230
01:13:47,495 --> 01:13:50,214
I really think Vera
should give evidence, Monty.

1231
01:13:50,215 --> 01:13:52,365
Oh, that's ridiculous.

1232
01:13:52,455 --> 01:13:55,054
She can no more
be called as a witness than I can.

1233
01:13:55,055 --> 01:13:57,054
